data: {"seq":73,"ts":"2026-08-22T15:57:47.812Z","text":"Let me now map the md5s from attack surface to binaries:\n- 23e3796091277a8be1e69dc8f5093dbd — the binary with main@0x407ff8, get_duid, dhcp6c? Actually get_duid is a DHCPv6 concept. dhcp6c/dhcp6s both deal with DUID. The path mentions \"main（0x407ff8）中 memcpy 使用 getaddrinfo 结果，get_duid 链至 sub_40F0F8\" — dhcp6c or dhcp6s. Let me check md5 mapping.\n- 08b0860102c4b034bfbae2238115a7e6 — has start_pppd and network_thread. Could be bpalogin? Or athadhoc?\n- 013126995a8c8228fc47c9d7a6ca9699 — ff_udp_set_remote_url etc. — could be minidlnad? No... ff_ prefix = \"fast forward\" media lib? Or maybe it's the \"ipcserver\"? Hmm, minidlnad is DLNA. Not likely.\n- 9582aa23b0bc2de099b9997b09457d01 — httpd (4882 functions).\n- 0b3a37ef404b079bb6442a19b5fd597b — ipcserver.\n- 53a5015af68ecf517285cb18d3aeab27 — athadhoc.\n\nLet me check firmware browse for each binary's md5. Actually easier: fw_routes with binary_md5 for httpd, and fw_search to identify binaries. Let me verify which binary has md5 013126... and 08b086... and 23e379...\n\nI can use fw_browse_firmware to look at the extracted files and compute md5s? No, I can't compute md5 via browse. But the attack surface evidence says \"decompiled firmware/.../dhcp6c md5=...\" only for surfaces. Let me try fw_get_function_source on those md5s to see which binary they belong to — the tool likely returns function source; maybe I can infer from the binary path. Actually, the tool needs job_id + md5 + addr only. Hmm.\n\nAlternative: use fw_routes with binary_md5? It needs binary_md5. Let me try fw_routes without binary_md5 to list all routes with binary info.\n\nLet me also check AS-AUTH docs. But first, the top path is 165af590169e4972 (dhcp6c or dhcp6s? get_duid → sub_40F0F8 → vsprintf + system). dhcp6c is a client, dhcp6s is a server. The identification says dhcp6c and dhcp6s are TCP with port null — those are probably wrong (dhcp6 uses UDP 546/547), but whatever.\n\nWait: path 165af590169e4972's binary has get_duid which is DHCPv6-specific. get_duid exists in dhcp6c (KAME dhcp6c). And sub_40F0F8 with vsprintf + system — in KAME dhcp6c, there's \"script\" execution via system(). Actually dhcp6c executes /etc/dhcp6c-script. There's a known vuln in dhcp6c: command injection via option processing? The args for dhcp6c: `dhcp6c [options] interface`. Options include `-f` (config file), `-c configfile`, `-d`, `-D`, `-r` (rapid commit?)... In KAME dhcp6c, `-r` is \"request certain information\" and there's also `-i`? Let me read the main function.\n\nLet me fetch the function sources for the top paths:\n1. dhcp6c/dhcp6s binary 23e379... main 0x407ff8, sink sub_40F0F8 @ 0x40f0f8.\n2. Binary 08b086... start_pppd 0x404db0, network_thread 0x4114d0.\n3. Binary 013126... ff_udp_set_remote_url 0x7590c, udp_open 0x75b20, ff_rtsp_connect 0x6d968.\n4. minidlnad? Actually ff_* functions — could be \"libffmpeg\"? Wait maybe this binary is \"minidlnad\"? No. Let me check: 013126... could be ipcserver? No, ipcserver md5 is 0b3a37ef. Let me look at remaining input binaries: dnsproxy, dropbearkey, scp, smbpasswd, uclited, vsftpd, dropbear, samba_multicall. The ff_* prefix (ff_udp_set_remote_url, ff_rtsp_connect, ff_http_init_auth_state) strongly suggests \"ffmpeg\" fork — maybe this is actually the \"ipcserver\"? Hmm no.\n\nActually, maybe it's \"ucamera\" or something in Archer C7? Archer C7 is a router... but there's \"minidlnad\" which uses avformat (libavformat.so.54). The ff_* functions might be inside minidlnad which links libavformat. minidlnad md5 unknown. Let me check by requesting function source with md5=013126... addr=0x7590c and see if the returned data gives binary name... it won't.\n\nBetter: fw_routes may map. Or fw_browse_firmware to list files and read... can't md5.\n\nAlternatively the attack-surface JSON files map md5 to binary. AS-008 is minidlnad. Let me fetch AS-008 and AS-003/005/011 to get md5s. Actually easier: fetch all remaining surfaces quickly? That's a lot of calls. Let me fetch AS-008 (minidlnad), AS-002 (bpalogin), AS-003 (dhcp6c), AS-004 (dhcp6s), AS-005 (dnsproxy), AS-011 (uclited), AS-010 (smbpasswd), AS-012 (vsftpd), AS-013 (dropbear), AS-015 (samba_multicall), AS-009 (scp), AS-006 (dropbearkey).\n\nActually the evidence field contains \"decompiled ... md5=...\". So each surface file tells me the md5 of its binary. Let me fetch the rest of surfaces in parallel to map all md5s, and also fetch the key function sources in parallel.","stream":true,"block":0}

