data: {"seq":300,"ts":"2026-08-22T15:59:09.184Z","text":"The httpd here is the classic TP-Link \"httpd\" RPM architecture (httpRpmConfAdd, httpRpmFs, LoginRpmHtm, GoIndex). The dispatch tables are in data (strings). The most productive approach for TP-Link httpd is to look at actual handler functions. But with no route table and data-only URLs, I need to find the config init function that registers all the RPM pages (the .htm paths). Let me find httpRpmConfInit caller (in_degree 1), i.e., where the full RPM config table is defined (a giant function that calls httpRpmConfInit + httpRpmConfAdd with many URL strings). Let me call trace httpRpmConfInit 'in' to find it — that giant config function contains the URL→function table; its decompilation would expose the strings and handler functions.\n\nAlternatively, more fruitful: examine sub_42595C (auth level 2 branch) and LoginRpmHtm, GoIndex. Also sub_425104 (credential check for login) — buffer overflow in password handling is a classic.\n\nBut honestly, the more effective play here: focus on **memory-corruption/command-injection on UDP services** where input is raw datagram and parsing is quick — those are easier to fuzz/trace? The qemu trace harness is HTTP GET based... Let me check how fw_request_trace works practically. It takes binary_md5 + argv + port + request_path. For httpd, argv might be [\"httpd\"] and request_path \"/login.htm\" — plausible. For UDP daemons, request_path would be meaningless. Hmm. But maybe the harness just runs the binary with argv and sends an HTTP GET to port — for daemons that don't speak HTTP it'd fail. So the dynamic verifier works best for httpd (and maybe minidlnad which speaks HTTP!).\n\nminidlnad (UPnP AV media server, HTTP on port 8200 typically): Process_upnphttp handles HTTP requests — GetGeo... etc. URL params → handlers like SendResp_resizedimg, SendResp_caption... These take URL params and do file ops — good candidates for command injection via path (e.g., uses system(\"...\") for image resizing? The dispatchers list \"sub_405594 sys_run_command\" and \"sub_405D5C sys_run_command_2\" and \"sub_416168 sys_run_command_3\" in minidlnad!). So minidlnad has cmd-exec wrappers reachable from HTTP requests. That's a prime target: minidlnad HTTP (DLNA) with command injection via URL param.\n\nAlso the ff_* libavformat functions are in the minidlnad's dependency chain (libavformat.so). udp_open/ff_udp_set_remote_url get URL from SQLite DB of media items? Probably not network.\n\nLet me pivot: examine minidlnad sys_run_command functions (0x405594, 0x405d5c, 0x416168) and their callers. And check the miniupnpd-like HTTP handler Process_upnphttp (0x40...?). Wait, minidlnad is a DLNA server; its HTTP service is on port 8200 (minidlna.conf). The identification says port null but minidlnad.conf probably","stream":true,"block":0}

