data: {"seq":338,"ts":"2026-08-22T15:59:26.822Z","text":"Analysis of minidlnad sub_405594: `snprintf(v5, 0x2000, \"rm -rf %s/art_cache\", &db_path); system(v5);` — db_path is from config (minidlna.conf db_dir), set at startup, not network. Not a network-visible cmdi.\n\nInteresting: pppd_b4483e71 is also decompiled in this job (pppd binary was excluded from inputs as not-public, but it's in the graph). And vsftpd sub_416168, etc.\n\nLet me now think about what's most valuable, given time budget (traces max 8, fuzz max 3).\n\nTarget choice rationale:\n1. httpd (port 80) — the most attacker-reachable. Full dispatch tables in data — hard to enumerate URLs statically, but historic TP-Link httpd vulnerabilities are abundant. The most famous for this era: the \"web\" handling of /userRpm/* with auth bypass, or the `httpRpmFs` path traversal, or the login handling overflow. Also, this httpd looks like the newer \"netatop/...\" style with httpRpmConfAdd — similar to TP-Link's httpd in Archer C5/C7 v1... There's CVE-2021-... for TP-Link... I should ground in code, not memory.\n\n2. dhcp6s (UDP 547) — P0 scored path; the inbound handler sub_4065B4 parses packets; potential option-parsing memory bug; plus the scored vsprintf+system wrapper seems not network-reachable. But packet parsing memory corruption is plausible (KAME dhcp6 had actual CVEs: e.g., \"dhcp6c/dhcp6s denial of service\"; and there's the CVE-2017-... \"KAME DHCP6\" ?). Also this TP-Link fork modified the code heavily (relay handling with sub_402E7C auth etc.).\n\n3. minidlnad (HTTP port 8200) — UPnP/DLNA; has sys_run_command wrappers (one at 0x405594 uses db_path; let me check the other two: 0x405d5c and 0x416168). Also buffer handling in SendResp_* (thumbnail/album art paths from URL params → path traversal / overflow). \n\n4. uclited (3197 functions! big binary; UPnP control point; also HTTP mini server on some port) — the \"StartMiniServer\" handler; historically TP-Link uclited had LAN HTTP server vulns.\n\nGiven the trace harness can do HTTP GET, the best candidates for verified dynamic evidence: httpd and minidlnad. Let me verify the harness by requesting a baseline trace for httpd to see if it works. Before that, I need httpd argv. TP-Link httpd runs as `httpd -p 80`? or with config `/etc/...`. Let me check the firmware's rcS / start script. etc/ is empty per browse? Let me browse \"firmware/binwalk_extracted/.../squashfs-root/etc\". The browse root is the job root; paths are relative to extraction root which includes \"firmware/...\". The earlier error","stream":true,"block":0}

