f httpd fails at bind, the trace could still show code coverage up to the failure... Crashes or behavior diff would be minimal. The trace uses a \"trigger HTTP GET\" — maybe it drives with a stub. Let me try.\n\nActually, before burning a trace, let me be more surgical: find an actual memory-corruption bug in httpd's pre-auth parser (e.g., httpLineUnescape, httpBase64decode, httpPwdDecode) with a crisp call chain, then verify with the trace.\n\nhttpPwdDecode: \n```c\nint httpPwdDecode(const char *a1, const char *a2, __int16 a3)\n{\n  v3 = a3;\n  if ( a3 >= word_5C21BA )\n  {\n    v5 = httpBase64decode(a1, a2, a3);\n    ...\n```\nIt guards with a3 (buffer size) — looks OK-ish. word_5C21BA is probably 8 (min pwd length?). Hmm.\n\nLet me look at httpBase64decode @ 0x42faf4: classic TP-Link base64 decode can overflow. And httpLineUnescape @ 0x432fc0: URL-decode into fixed buffer — classic overflow. And the multipart parser httpMimeParse @ 0x444940.\n\nAlso there's this 0x400e28 binary with many strcpy in main — let me identify it. And deep-dive xl2tpd network_thread str","stream":true,"block":0}

