data: {"seq":27,"ts":"2026-08-22T16:04:12.700Z","text":"The user wants me to continue: for httpd's suspected command injection / login paths, do dynamic verification (qemu differential or fuzz), and only record findings after getting dynamic results.\n\nPlan:\n1. First, I need concrete httpd attack hypotheses. From static analysis so far:\n   - httpPwdDecode (0x443918): httpBase64decode(a1, a2, a3) — length checked vs v3 (a3 <= decoded len?). Looks OK-ish: decode into a2 with limit a3? httpBase64decode signature unknown; the guard `v5 <= 0 || v3 < v5` returns -1. So bounded.\n   - LoginRpmHtm → login handling; auth gate function sub_425AA4. The credential compare sub_425104 → sub_4250A0 (timing state machine).\n   \n2. I haven't found a strong httpd memory corruption yet. Let me look at:\n   - httpBase64decode (0x42faf4) — the cyclic buffer decode: TP-Link's httpBase64decode historically writes without bounds check (decodes into output buffer of size = input size... it's fine if caller passes buffer).\n   - The dispatcher path: httpLineUnescape on query strings.\n   - Also the \"httpGetEnv\" → query parsing (sub_420B48?). Let me look for the query-string parser that fills the \"Env\" — TP-Link's is `httpGetEnv`/`EnvGetValue` with fixed buffer copy → classic overflow in `strcpy(envvalue, query)`.\n\nActually, a well-known family of TP-Link httpd vulns in this code base: the \"net-cgi\" or the \"GetPassword\" ... hmm, let me not speculate. Let me find httpGetEnv and its parsing to assess query-string handling memory safety.\n\n3. Command injection in httpd: which httpd functions call system()? Let me find via fw_attack_surface with source=\"network\" sink=\"cmdexec\" and look for httpd md5 paths. Alternatively, search httpd for functions whose callees include system: fw_search pattern \"system\" might find \"sub_x\" names? Search by pattern \"system\" with label Function in httpd? fw_search searches function names; many wrapper functions named \"sub_...\" won't reveal. But some exported functions like \"sysRunCmd\"? Let me try searching \"RunCmd\" or \"exec\".\n\nLet me do:\n- fw_search pattern \"system\" limit 100 (functions).\n- fw_attack_surface? Can't filter by binary. But I can list paths with sink filter: source=\"network\", sink=\"cmdexec\", verified_only false, brief, limit 50 — the top results may include httpd.\n\nAlso, for dynamic verification: the trace harness — let me try fw_request_trace for httpd: binary_md5=9582aa23..., argv=[], port=80, request_path=\"/\" or \"/userRpm/LoginRpm.htm\". This tests whether the harness can run httpd under qemu-user. Note httpd as a daemon binds port 80 and does select(); under qemu-user with an HTTP GET to port 80... The harness presumably does that. Let me try.\n\nAlso check what qemu-user does with a MIPS httpd — it needs the mips binary and its shared libs — should be fine.\n\nLet me first find httpd cmdexec candidates statically (search functions calling system), then run traces.\n\nOrder:\n1. fw_search \"system\" (functions) — find httpd wrappers.\n2. fw_search \"exec\" pattern.\n3. fw_get_function_source httpBase64decode 0x42faf4 (bounds?) — maybe skip; first get the two searches and httpGetEnv.\n\nActually let me be efficient: search functions with label Function and pattern \"http\" in httpd? Too many. Let me search \"system\" and \"Run\" separately.","stream":true,"block":0}

