data: {"seq":133,"ts":"2026-08-22T16:12:28.187Z","text":"The DDNS path: swSetDynDdnsCfg → ucSetDynDdnsCfg. uc* functions typically write to shared memory or send IPC to uclited. dynStart spawns a task sub_53513C (a thread) — upnp soap client probably (HTTP). startNoip spawns sub_53E14C thread. These don't call system() in httpd (they're socket-based ddns clients). Sub-thread functions (sub_53513C etc.) might call system with domain, e.g., \"nslookup dimain\" — need to check. But these are spawned only *after* login+save (authenticated). Command injection there would be post-auth (still valid: post-auth RCE is a real finding but lower severity).\n\nHonestly, given budget constraints, the most productive target remains the raw packet parsers for UDP services (dnsproxy / dhcp6s / bpalogin) for memory corruption, plus fuzz them. The harness now works for qemu (the httpd trace produced baseline coverage with 30 funcs), which is promising for fuzz runs too.\n\nLet me reconsider the plan given the user instruction: \"对 httpd 上已经看到的可疑命令注入/登录路径做动态验证，拿到 qemu 差分或 fuzz 结果后再决定是否入库。\" So they want:\n1. Dynamic verification of httpd's suspicious command-injection/login paths (qemu diff or fuzz).\n2. Then decide whether to record findings.\n\nSo: I should get a *working* httpd trace that shows diff coverage on login path, or a fuzz crash. The \"/\" trace attempt got 409 (another trace running — maybe the previous one was still being cleaned up). Retry now.\n\nAlso, for the login path, the connection reset on /userRpm/LoginRpm.htm might be because httpd's per-connection handler died. To make the diff meaningful, try:\n- request_path \"/\" — see if trigger_result gives bytes/status (if it returns HTML, harness works and LoginRpm.htm reset is interesting).\n- If \"/\" works, then LoginRpm.htm reset is likely a crash in login processing → strong signal worth fuzzing the pwd decode.\n\nIf \"/\" also resets → harness/network issue (","stream":true,"block":0}

