data: {"seq":166,"ts":"2026-08-22T16:12:50.501Z","text":"The ddns client threads use phInitModule (Peanut Hull / dyn-dns HTTP clients) — no direct system in these. Confirmed: DDNS updates are socket-based. OK, the \"command injection\" in httpd isn't obvious via DDNS.\n\nNow retry the httpd trace \"/\" — check whether the trace slot freed up. Then think about what the connection reset means.\n\nActually, let me think about httpd under qemu-user: httpd main → httpd() (the server) → binds port 80 with SO_REUSEADDR, forks/threads per connection (wmnet). Under qemu-user in the docker sandbox, the GET is presumably sent to 127.0.0.1:80 after httpd binds. The ConnectionResetError suggests the connection was accepted then closed abruptly. Possible: httpd under qemu-user crashes on the recv because libc/msglog writes to /dev/console... or the wmnet socket group wait... Since baseline itself only covered 30 functions (mostly startup), maybe the accept loop runs in a *forked child* that qemu-user's coverage isn't following, then the child crashed.\n\nAlternative hypothesis: httpd requires 2 requests (it sends a redirect to /userRpm/Index.htm) — but reset (not 302) argues crash.\n\nHonestly the trace harness's HTTP GET against this httpd is not productive. The guide says empty diff is not a vuln. So per the user's instruction \"拿到 qemu 差分或 fuzz 结果后再决定是否入库\" — for httpd login path, we may not get a clean diff; then","stream":true,"block":0}

