data: {"seq":224,"ts":"2026-08-22T16:13:29.722Z","text":"dhcp6_get_options (dhcp6s 0x40dc1c): parses wire options. Lengths are checked per option (v9 = v5+v7 and `if (a2 < v5+v7)` — wait, v5 = a1+4 first; and bound check: `if (a2 < (unsigned int)&v5[v7])` → per option-end bound against end pointer a2 (which is `&byte_43CE7C[v0]` in server6_recv = end of packet). OK.\n\nPotential issue: `case 1` (client ID): v64=v7 (16-bit sign-extended!), v65=v5. duidcpy(a3, &v64) — a3 is the option array; duidcpy copies up to v64 bytes? Need to see duidcpy — it likely allocates len bytes and copies. `v64` is `signed int` — v7 is unsigned __int16 up to 65535; cast to int stays positive. duidcpy(a3, &v64) reads struct {len, ptr}. Probably fine (allocates).\n\n`case 11` (auth): `a3[53] = v7 - 31; v47 = malloc(v7-31); memcpy(v47, src, a3[53])` — src = v5+11. v7 checked >= 0xB and if v7 != 11 then (v7-11) >= 0x14 → v7-31 >= 0. OK.\n\n`sub_40CBA8` (case 33, 21, 24, 29, 30) and `sub_40D3D4` (case 34, 22, 23, 27, 28, 31) — these parse addresses/lifetimes. Let me examine sub_40CBA8 and sub_40D3D4 for unchecked memcpy/strcpy — these handle SIP/domain lists etc. In KAME dhcp6s, option 21-30 are SIP server domain names / addresses, NIS domains... TP-Link added these.\n\nAlso `sub_40D578` (IA_NA/IA_PD sub-options parse → handles IAADDR with lifetime; classic bug: memcpy of address).\n\nGiven the huge audit surface, the smartest dynamic play is function-level fuzz of dhcp6_get_options (dhcp6s md5 23e379... addr 0x40dc1c) with args [buf,len] — the harness calls it with attacker-controlled buf. If there's an overflow (e.g., in duidcpy or sub_40CBA8), AFL++ will find a crash.\n\nBut wait — the fuzz tool: \"function (hex address like 0x131b4) (omit for whole-binary mode)\"; args: \"[buf,len]\". So it fuzzes a function with a synthesized call (data args from fuzz). dhcp6_get_options(buf, len, optinfo) — 3 args; the third is an output struct array (must be valid memory). The harness probably fills len = buf size; extra args? We can only spec 2 args. Maybe it passes NULL for the rest → crash/no.\n\nBetter fuzz targets (2 args simple):\n- sub_40CBA8(len, buf, code, out) — 4 args; not ideal.\n- duidcpy(dst, src) — 2 args: (dst is 16-byte array? no: duidcpy(a3 /*size_t* array*/, &v64)). Hmm.\n- httpBase64decode(a1, a2, a3) — 3 args but a2 output buffer required.\n\nHonestly, for the fuzz tool, typical usage: single-arg or (buf,len) functions. Let me look for simple parse functions with (buf,len) signature:\n- dhcp6c/dhcp6s base64_decodestring? \n- dnsproxy dns_decode_request(a1) — single arg (whole packet struct). Not (buf,len).\n- bpalogin's extract_valueINT2 / receive_transaction parsing.\n\nAlternative: whole-binary fuzz mode (omit function) — fuzzes the whole binary with argv input? That's heavier but real. Hmm \"whole-binary mode\" — then args spec applies to argv maybe.\n\nGiven max 3 fuzz runs per session, plan:\n1. Use one fuzz run on httpd httpBase64decode? Not the poppiest.\n2. Better: run fuzz on dhcp6_get_options (dhcp6s) with [buf,len] — if the harness passes optinfo as zeroed output (perhaps it sets arg2 = len and arg0 = buf, arg1?), spec says e.g. [buf,len] means arg0=buf, arg1=len. Extra params (a3) uninitialized → likely crash at start (writing to a3+...). Risky but a crash from bad arg setup is indistinguishable from a real bug — must verify crash location. Might burn budget.\n\nSafer: find a self-contained (buf,len) parser. Let me check:\n- libavformat ff_udp_set_remote_url(ffmt_ctx, url) — 2 args (ctx ptr, url str)? args [ctx? ,buf]? not (buf,len).\n- minidlnad parsexml / ParseNameValue.\n- vsftpd str_split_char_reverse...\n\nHmm, actually bpalogin! It's UDP","stream":true,"block":0}

