ingle-arg: args [\"buf\"]. The unescape bounds-checked; probably no crash.\n\nGiven the uncertainty, better to spend the next fuzz run on something guaranteed self-contained: whole-binary fuzz? Or the dhcp6_get_options with [buf,len] where a3 might be... Actually look: in dhcp6_get_options, a3 is used heavily immediately (a3+14 etc.) — a NULL a3 crashes instantly on first option. If the harness passes 0, useless.\n\nLet me reconsider: maybe the harness's function-fuzz is \"whole function fuzz\" — it runs the function repeatedly with mutations of a fuzz buffer passed as arg0 and length arg1, and *the harness allocates remaining pointer args as zeroed buffers* — many such harnesses do treat the last arg as an output struct. We don't know. The API description: \"Request a function-level AFL++ fuzz run (dynamic, arm/mips via qemu persistent hook). Only with a concrete hypothesis.\"; args \"arg spec e.g. [buf,len]\". So the harness knows the function signature from the code graph and maps \"buf\" to the buffer arg and \"len\" to the length arg. For dhcp6_get_options(buf=a1 unsigned char*, len=a2 unsigned int, optinfo=a3 size_t*) — spec [\"buf\",\"len\"] should work if harness maps by name/position. Then a3 remains whatever default (likely NULL) → crash at a3[14]... hmm that's immediate invalid write → any fuzz input \"crashes\" → false positive. Unless harness zero-fills a3... unknown.\n\nI think the safest concrete-fuzz target with a single (buf,len)-like signature and self-contained output is:\n\n- dhcp6s","stream":true,"block":0}

