data: {"seq":279,"ts":"2026-08-22T16:14:23.505Z","text":"bpalogin receive_udp_transaction: recvfrom into a3 with max 0x5DC (1500), then sub_402870(a1, a3, v13) processes. sub_402870 is the transaction parser (probably \"receive_transaction\"), it decides based on first u16. Then login/logout handle. Let me check the deeper parse in login (0x401ab8) since command injection via bpalogin config (it runs pppd daemon on success!). bpalogin launches pppd with auth details — classic places for cmd injection via session fields.\n\nBut honestly, bpalogin is \"not-public\"?? No — bpalogin is IN-002 (udp, public). Port unknown. It's the Broadband Phone (VoIP) login agent? \"bpalogin\" = Broadband Phone... The .conf might set port. It's a UDP client that authenticates a VoIP line and then runs pppd. Its \"external input\" is the authentication server's UDP replies — attacker in the network path (same LAN as a MITM) could inject. Attack surface score is lower.\n\nThe trace slot STILL 409s. Maybe the runner is doing something long (like a fuzz run queued by another session?). I'll keep retrying periodically.\n\nGiven the user's explicit push (\"对 httpd 上已经看到的可疑命令注入/登录路径做动态验证，拿到 qemu 差分或 fuzz 结果后再决定是否入库\"), they want a *decision* after dynamic results. If the httpd trace keeps failing to produce diff (reset), that's itself a signal to try fuzz on a concrete httpd function. Best httpd fuzz candidates with simple signatures:\n\n1. httpBase64decode(a1, a2, a3) — 3 params; fuzz harness in \"function mode\" with args [buf,len] probably sets arg0=buf (input), arg1=len, arg2=?? maybe 0. a2 would be NULL → write to NULL → crash. Not useful.\n\nHmm, wait — maybe the fuzz harness does something smarter: for signature (char*, char*, int) with args spec [buf,len], it allocates buf of len bytes and passes as arg0, len as arg1, and the remaining args as NULL/0? Unknown. Risky.\n\n2. httpLineUnescape(char *a1) — 1 param; in-place unescape. Args spec [\"buf\"]? The example only shows [buf,len]. Single-arg: args [\"buf\"]. The unescape bounds-checked; probably no crash.\n\nGiven the uncertainty, better to spend the next fuzz run on something guaranteed self-contained: whole-binary fuzz? Or the dhcp6_get_options with [buf,len] where a3 might be... Actually look: in dhcp6_get_options, a3 is used heavily immediately (a3+14 etc.) — a NULL a3 crashes instantly on first option. If the harness passes 0, useless.\n\nLet me reconsider: maybe the harness's function-fuzz is \"whole function fuzz\" — it runs the function repeatedly with mutations of a fuzz buffer passed as arg0 and length arg1, and *the harness allocates remaining pointer args as zeroed buffers* — many such harnesses do treat the last arg as an output struct. We don't know. The API description: \"Request a function-level AFL++ fuzz run (dynamic, arm/mips via qemu persistent hook). Only with a concrete hypothesis.\"; args \"arg spec e.g. [buf,len]\". So the harness knows the function signature from the code graph and maps \"buf\" to the buffer arg and \"len\" to the length arg. For dhcp6_get_options(buf=a1 unsigned char*, len=a2 unsigned int, optinfo=a3 size_t*) — spec [\"buf\",\"len\"] should work if harness maps by name/position. Then a3 remains whatever default (likely NULL) → crash at a3[14]... hmm that's immediate invalid write → any fuzz input \"crashes\" → false positive. Unless harness zero-fills a3... unknown.\n\nI think the safest concrete-fuzz target with a single (buf,len)-like signature and self-contained output is:\n\n- dhcp6s `duidcpy(dst, src)` — no.\n- `sub_40CBA8(len, buf, code, out)` — arg0=len? no.\n\nLet me instead inspect sub_40CBA8 / sub_40D3D4 to see whether there's an actual overflow; if there is, and the function is self-contained (takes buf,len and out), we can fuzz with [buf,len]. Even if out is NULL by default, hmm.\n\nAlternative clean approach: **fuzz run on whole binary** mode for bpalogin or dnsproxy with argv? The tool with function omitted = whole-binary mode; args probably = argv spec. Whole-binary fuzz of a UDP daemon via AFL qemu — requires the daemon to read from stdin? Probably the harness feeds fuzz data as argv/stdio. Unpredictable.\n\nOK here's the pragmatic path given constraints and instructions:\n1. The user asked to verify httpd login/命令注入 paths dynamically and only then decide recording. We now have:\n   - httpd trace baseline OK; trigger GET to LoginRpm.htm → connection reset, 0 diff. This is *observed* evidence that the login handler path isn't exercising (and the reset happens even on \"/\" attempts... can't confirm yet).\n2. Need to nail at least ONE concrete finding. The strongest *concrete* ones so far:\n   a. dhcp6s sub_40F0F8 (vsprintf→system) — but callers use fixed strings; NOT network-reachable. Won't record.\n   b. httpPwdDecode → bounded. Not a vuln.\n   c. httpLineUnescape → bounded. Not a vuln.\n   \nSo my httpd login/命令注入 hunt hasn't produced a concrete bug. I need to identify a REAL vuln first, then verify dynamically.\n\nLet me take a fresh look at the httpd surface for memory bugs in request parsing. Actually — big idea: check `httpGenListCaseFind` and the query-string parser (`httpQueryParse`?). Let me find where the query string is tokenized into the env list: probably `httpQueryParse`/`httpParse`/sub_444940 httpMimeParse or sub_41... Search \"Query\" in httpd.\n\nAlso check sub_420430 (called by main right before httpd()) and httpBasicRpmInit.\n\nAlso — known TP-Link vuln class in this exact httpd generation (C7 v2 3.15.4): \"GET /userRpm/... HTTP/1.1\" with long URL crashes httpd (stack overflow via httpRpmFsA path handling / strcpy of URL into fixed buffer). httpRpmFsA has `strcpy` in bt! `httpRpmFsA(a1, path)` — the path arg. In httpDispatcher, a1+28 = path after the matched prefix... Let me read httpRpmFsA full source (0x426524, in_degree 128!! — many handlers call it with path). It has strcpy(buf, ...) in its bt list: `httpBufferGet a1 httpServerUncompress httpMimeContentEncodingGet strcpy strcat a2 open strstr` — likely strcpy(path_buf, a2) where a2 = URL-suffix pointer from the request. If path_buf fixed and a2 attacker-long → overflow!\n\nRead httpRpmFsA full: signature (int a1, const char *a2) — a2 is the .htm path (fixed internally) — from httpDispatcher: `v14 (a1+48)`... hmm in LoginRpmHtm: httpRpmFsA(a1, \"/userRpm/LoginRpm.htm\") — constant. The dispatcher","stream":true,"block":0}

