data: {"seq":66,"ts":"2026-08-22T16:16:14.159Z","text":"\"/\" also resets with 0 bytes — identical behavior to LoginRpm.htm. So the connection reset is a **harness/article artifact** (httpd under qemu-user accepts and immediately resets — probably because httpd's threaded accept model or the socket handling under qemu-user doesn't complete the handshake for the harness's GET, or httpd requires two-step handshake). Not evidence of a crash in the login path specifically.\n\nSo: dynamic verification via httpd traces shows *no request processing* (0 diff) — the harness cannot drive httpd's request loop meaningfully under this setup. That's a harness limitation, not a bug.\n\nPer the instructions: \"观测到/已验证必须带真实 trace_id\" and \"空差分不是漏洞\"; I should NOT record an httpd finding claiming observed/verified reachability based on these traces. And static analysis didn't yield a concrete httpd memory-corruption or cmdi in the login path with a call chain to a sink.\n\nNow decide: record findings only with real evidence. What have we got with real evidence?\n\n1. dhcp6s P0 path (main→get_duid→sub_40F168→sub_40F0F8→system):\n   - Static chain exists (call trace shows get_duid and sub_40F168 calling sub_40F0F8; get_duid called from main).\n   - BUT the strings passed are constants: sub_40F168(\"[%s]wan_if:%s\\n\",\"get_duid\",aEth02) → echo into /dev/ttyS0. aEth02 = global string (likely \"eth0.2\" or from config/interface). Not network-controlled. So it's NOT an exploitable cmdi; it's a debugging sink. Recording this as \"P0 cmdi\" would be a false positive. Don't record as finding. (hint only)\n\n2. xl2tpd start_pppd: argv-based execv, not system. Not cmdi. network_thread strcpy — in xl2tpd which is excluded from public inputs (not-public). Skip.\n\n3. libavformat memcpy(ai_addrlen): sizes bounded by getaddrinfo results (16/28). Not exploitable; AI-reviewed as P1 but actually SAFE (ai_addrlen for IPv4=16, IPv6=28; the context structs likely have room). Don't record.\n\nSo currently: no confirmed vulnerability with a concrete sink reachable from a public input has emerged from deep-dive. Per the discipline, I must not record speculative findings.\n\nRemaining budget: traces used: ef55266d1f34 (ok), 2ba400f78c44 (ok) → 6 more available. Fuzz broken.\n\nWhere can I still get REAL dynamic evidence of a bug? Options:\nA. Try trace on other binaries that actually process HTTP GET: **minidlnad** now that qemu can run things (the earlier failure was \"unsafe library search path\" from an ELF RPATH — the fix might have addressed it; retry minidlnad trace! DLNA HTTP GET \"/\" should exercise Process_upnphttp → real diff vs baseline!). minidlnad also has the `rm -rf %s/art_cache` system() (db_path from config only, not network) and sub_416168 system(\"isgnsta ...\") with integer ip. Not cmdi, but minidlnad HTTP parsing is a real parser worth diffing. And critically — minidlnad stays in foreground and serves HTTP → the harness's GET may actually reach the request loop → diff! Let me retry minidlnad trace (argv [\"-f\",\"/tmp/minidlna.conf\"]? minidlnad needs a config; without one it uses defaults and missing db_path → '/tmp/mediaserver/cache' path — mkdir/access fails maybe but server still starts? It needs an interface? It binds to port 8200 on 0.0.0.0 by default. Let's try argv [\"-d\"] (debug) or none. Actually main requires argc>=2? No: for loop `for (i=2; i<a1; ++i)` — argc 1 works; defaults apply. Let me just try argv [] port 8200 path \"/\".\n\nB. If minidlnad diff works, use it to verify a minidlnad memory bug. What concrete bug do we have in minidlnad? Candidates: the media directory realpath handling (`realpath(v24, v43)` with v","stream":true,"block":0}

