or the media handlers. v8 with 7 bytes is... suspicious. Actually wait — maybe v8 is meant to be the \"URL\" and the loop limit is 511 because v8 was *supposed* to alias a bigger region? Let me re-read:\n\n```\nchar v7[16];   // method\nchar v8[7];    // URL?? no...\nchar v9[2];    // /icons/ handler arg\nchar v10;      // /Resized/\nchar v11[2];   // /AlbumArt/ /Captions/\nchar v12[500]; // /MediaItems/ /Thumbnails/ handler arg\n```\n\nHandlers: SendResp_thumbnail(a1, v12), SendResp_albumArt(a1, v11), SendResp_resizedimg(a1, &v10), SendResp_icon(a1, v9), SendResp_caption(a1, v11), SendResp_dlnafile(a1, v12). And v8 → passed to sub_40D050(a1, v8)/sub_40CCC8(a1,v8) for SUBSCRIBE/UNSUBSCRIBE — notification URL.\n\nHmm wait, actually re-reading: the URL loop writes v8... Actually hold on. Let me re-read more carefully:\n\n```\nif ( !strncmp(v5, \"http://\", 7u) ) { for ( v5 += 7; *v5 != 47; ++v5 ) ; }\nfor ( i = 0; i < 511 && *v5 != 32 && *v5 != 13; ++i )\n  v8[i] = *v5++;\nv8[i] = 0;\n```\n\nThis copies the URL path into v8 — 7 bytes! Any URL path longer than 6 chars overflows v8. But wait — is that right? \"/\" is 1 char → fits. \"/rootDesc.xml\" is 13 chars → write 13 bytes + NUL to v8[7] → overflow by 7 bytes into v9/v10/v11/v12. The stack layout: v8[7] sp+50..sp+56, v9[2] sp+57..58 (v9[0]=sp+57), v10 sp+59, v11[2] sp+5A..5B, v12[500] sp+5C..sp+2BF. So the overflow writes into subsequent locals; 511-byte URL would overflow v8→","stream":true,"block":0}

