data: {"seq":135,"ts":"2026-08-22T16:16:51.434Z","text":"sub_40D1FC is minidlnad's HTTP request parser (upnphttp.c). Important observation:\n\n```\nv8[7] (7 bytes!) — for HTTP URL: `for (i=0; i<511 && *v5!=32 && *v5!=13; ++i) v8[i] = *v5++;` — writes to v8 WHICH IS ONLY 7 BYTES but the loop limit is 511!!! \n```\n\nLook at the stack layout:\n```\nchar v8[7]; // [sp+50h] [+50h]\nchar v9[2]; // [sp+57h] [+57h]\nchar v10;    // [sp+59h] [+59h]\nchar v11[2]; // [sp+5Ah] [+5Ah]\nchar v12[500]; // [sp+5Ch] [+5Ch]\n```\nv8 is 7 bytes at sp+50h; v9 at sp+57, v10 at sp+59, v11 at sp+5A, v12 at sp+5C (500 bytes). The URL-copy loop writes into v8 for up to 511 bytes → **stack buffer overflow of v8[7]**, running over v9, v10, v11, v12 (500 bytes), and beyond! The URL (v8) is from the raw HTTP request line (attacker-controlled path up to first space or CR, up to 511 chars).\n\nWait — check the first loop: request method copied into v7[16]. The \"http://\" skip: if request starts with \"http://\"... Actually the parsing: v5 = request line (a1+32). First token = method (v7[16]). Then skip spaces. If v5 starts with \"http://\", advance past it (absolute URL form), then the *path* is copied into v8[7]-loop with 511 limit but 7-byte buffer → overflow!\n\nHold on, let me re-check: the loop writing v8 is the \"URL\" (second token of request line; for \"GET /path HTTP/1.1\" it's \"/path\"... wait no — after method, `while (*v5 == ' ') ++v5;` then `if (!strncmp(v5, \"http://\", 7))` ... then skip to '/'. Then loop copies from v5 into v8 up to 511 bytes. For \"GET / HTTP/1.1\", v5 = \"/ HTTP/1.1\", copies \"/\" then stops at space. v8 gets \"/\". But for a URL longer than 6 chars — e.g. \"/MediaItems/12345...\" — v8 (7 bytes) overflows immediately for any path longer than 6 bytes!\n\nWait, is v8 really only 7 bytes? `char v8[7]; // [sp+50h]`. Hmm, but then v12[500] at sp+5Ch is used as the *path buffer* passed to SendResp_thumbnail(a1, v12)! So v12 (500 bytes) is the intended URL buffer for the media handlers. v8 with 7 bytes is... suspicious. Actually wait — maybe v8 is meant to be the \"URL\" and the loop limit is 511 because v8 was *supposed* to alias a bigger region? Let me re-read:\n\n```\nchar v7[16];   // method\nchar v8[7];    // URL?? no...\nchar v9[2];    // /icons/ handler arg\nchar v10;      // /Resized/\nchar v11[2];   // /AlbumArt/ /Captions/\nchar v12[500]; // /MediaItems/ /Thumbnails/ handler arg\n```\n\nHandlers: SendResp_thumbnail(a1, v12), SendResp_albumArt(a1, v11), SendResp_resizedimg(a1, &v10), SendResp_icon(a1, v9), SendResp_caption(a1, v11), SendResp_dlnafile(a1, v12). And v8 → passed to sub_40D050(a1, v8)/sub_40CCC8(a1,v8) for SUBSCRIBE/UNSUBSCRIBE — notification URL.\n\nHmm wait, actually re-reading: the URL loop writes v8... Actually hold on. Let me re-read more carefully:\n\n```\nif ( !strncmp(v5, \"http://\", 7u) ) { for ( v5 += 7; *v5 != 47; ++v5 ) ; }\nfor ( i = 0; i < 511 && *v5 != 32 && *v5 != 13; ++i )\n  v8[i] = *v5++;\nv8[i] = 0;\n```\n\nThis copies the URL path into v8 — 7 bytes! Any URL path longer than 6 chars overflows v8. But wait — is that right? \"/\" is 1 char → fits. \"/rootDesc.xml\" is 13 chars → write 13 bytes + NUL to v8[7] → overflow by 7 bytes into v9/v10/v11/v12. The stack layout: v8[7] sp+50..sp+56, v9[2] sp+57..58 (v9[0]=sp+57), v10 sp+59, v11[2] sp+5A..5B, v12[500] sp+5C..sp+2BF. So the overflow writes into subsequent locals; 511-byte URL would overflow v8→v12 (500) → beyond sp+2C0 → smashing whatever is after (saved registers / return address above the frame at sp+... The frame seems upward (sp+offset), so locals are above sp; saved regs are likely below (lower addresses) — overflow upward hits other locals and anything above the frame (caller's frame). \n\nBut wait — is this reachable via HTTP? sub_40D1FC is called from Process_upnphttp (the HTTP connection handler) with a1 = connection struct containing the raw request in a1+32. The request line is attacker-controlled (URL path). So yes: **stack overflow in minidlnad HTTP request parsing — URL path > 6 chars** — hmm, but that would make minidlnad crash on literally any normal request (\"/rootDesc.xml\")! That can't be — minidlna serves /rootDesc.xml to every DLNA client... UNLESS the variable layout is mis-synthesized by Hex-Rays and v8 is actually bigger (e.g., 511 bytes starting at sp+50) and v9/v10/v11/v12 overlap part of v8!\n\nLook again: v9[2] at sp+57h, v10 at sp+59h, v11[2] at sp+5Ah, v12[500] at sp+5Ch. If v8 were declared `char v8[7]`, then v9 starts immediately after. But could the real source be: `char url[512]; path[500]`? The disassembly would tell us. The loop limit 511 strongly suggests the *intended* buffer is 512 bytes. And v12[500] is the *path* buffer for thumbnail etc. So perhaps decompilation merged two overlapping buffers (url v8 = part of v12? no...).\n\nActually, common MiniDLNA source (upnphttp.c, Process_upnphttp):\n```\nchar url[512]; // or 'v8'\n...\n/* copy the url */\nfor(i = 0; i < 511 && *c != ' ' && *c != '\\r'; i++)\n  url[i] = *c++;\nurl[i] = 0;\n```\nAnd SendResp_thumbnail takes 'const char * path'... In the real source, `url` is a 512-byte buffer, and the path passed to thumbnail handlers is the *same* url buffer (url + something). The v12[500] is probably how IDA sized the same buffer in a different sub-region — Hex-Rays showing overlapping locals.\n\nGiven v9/v10/v11/v12 all sit *inside* the 512-byte region starting at sp+50 (sp+50+511 = sp+2","stream":true,"block":0}

