ack overflow in minidlnad HTTP request parsing — URL path > 6 chars** — hmm, but that would make minidlnad crash on literally any normal request (\"/rootDesc.xml\")! That can't be — minidlna serves /rootDesc.xml to every DLNA client... UNLESS the variable layout is mis-synthesized by Hex-Rays and v8 is actually bigger (e.g., 511 bytes starting at sp+50) and v9/v10/v11/v12 overlap part of v8!\n\nLook again: v9[2] at sp+57h, v10 at sp+59h, v11[2] at sp+5Ah, v12[500] at sp+5Ch. If v8 were declared `char v8[7]`, then v9 starts immediately after. But could the real source be: `char url[512]; path[500]`? The disassembly would tell us. The loop limit 511 strongly suggests the *intended* buffer is 512 bytes. And v12[500] is the *path* buffer for thumbnail etc. So perhaps decompilation merged two overlapping buffers (url v8 = part of v12? no...).\n\nActually, common MiniDLNA source (upnphttp.c, Process_upnphttp):\n```\nchar url[512]; // or 'v8'\n...\n/* copy the url */\nfor(i = 0; i < 511 && *c != ' ' && *c != '\\r'; i++)\n  url[i] = *c++;\nurl[i] = 0;\n```\nAnd SendResp_thumbnail takes 'const char * path'... In the real source, `url` is a 512-byte buffer, and the path passed to thumbnail handlers is the *same* url buffer (url + something). The v12[500] is probably how IDA sized the same buffer in a different sub-region — Hex-Rays showing overlapping locals.\n\nGiven v9/v10/v11/v12 all sit *inside* the 512-byte region starting at sp+50 (sp+50+511 = sp+2BF < sp+5C+500=sp+298? wait v12 spans sp+5C..sp+2B8; and v8 511 bytes would span sp+50..sp+2BF → v12 (sp+5C..sp+2B8) is INSIDE). So it's very likely that in reality there's ONE 512-byte URL buffer (sp+50), and IDA split it into v8[7]+v9+v10+v11+v12[500]. The overflow is therefore a **decompiler artifact**, not a real bug. Good catch — do NOT record this.\n\nTo confirm: compare with next instruction v8[i]=0 buffer size... Anyway, classic IDA mis","stream":true,"block":0}

