or 2048. If URL tail > buffer - \"/tmp/\" length → strcat overflow → stack/heap overflow. But buffer is probably heap (allocated). An overflow into heap adjacent → corruption. BUT: the URL itself is stored somewhere; a1+20 points into... the request buffer? If a1+20 points at the same buffer httpBufferGet returns, then the tail is *inside* the destination buffer — strcat(v2, v3) where v3 is within v2's own buffer region → memmove-like self-overlap → could still overflow beyond v2's end if tail extends to buffer end.\n\nHmm, \"httpBufferGet(a1)\" — let me check what it returns and the buffer size. Also find where httpRpmFs is registered (which URL prefixes). Let me read httpBufferGet and search for \"httpRpmFs\" registration... The routes: e.g., /web/* static pages and other \"Rpm.htm\" fet","stream":true,"block":0}

