data: {"seq":325,"ts":"2026-08-22T16:18:50.436Z","text":"httpRpmUpload analysis:\n- Buffers: v6, src, v68 = 0x800 each; v69 = 0x40 (header buffer).\n- sub_45ADC4 parses a multipart header into v69 (0x40 buffer) — need to check its bound handling. If it writes the header (\"Content-Disposition: form-data; name=\\\"xx\\\"; filename=\\\"...\\\"\") into a 64-byte buffer → overflow with a long header. That's the classic TP-Link upload overflow! Let me read sub_45ADC4.\n- `if (v7 < uploadBufLenGet(a2))` — content length must be < upload buffer size, else error 413. uploadBufLenGet = upload buffer len (probably 0x20000 or so). So overall upload size bounded.\n\nLet me read sub_45ADC4 (header parse) — signature: (a1, v71=v56 ptr, v73=&v55, v72=&v57, v69=header buf). If the header buffer is 0x40 (64 bytes) and sub_45ADC4 copies the header line ≥64 bytes → overflow. Header = \"Content-Disposition: form-data; name=\"file\"; filename=\"<attacker>\"\". A long filename → long header → overflow of v69 (64B) on the heap (memPoolAlloc) → heap corruption. Post-auth (requires login session or auth bypass), and upload page requires auth. But the auth gate may be bypassable... The dispatcher calls sub_425CB8(a1, v6) for non-special handlers; if auth fails → LoginRpmHtm. So needs valid session (admin creds). Firm","stream":true,"block":0}

