data: {"seq":335,"ts":"2026-08-22T16:18:57.779Z","text":"sub_45ADC4: filename/name extraction is strncpy(dest, v18, v21) with `if ((unsigned int)(v21 - 1) >= 0x3F) goto LABEL_18;` → v21 (length) ≤ 0x3F+1=64, fits in v69 (0x40=64)... wait v21-1 < 0x40 → v21 ≤ 0x40; strncpy(dest, v18, v21); dest[v21]=0 — dest is 0x40 bytes; v21 ≤ 64 → writes dest[64]=0 → **1-byte overflow** (dest[64] past the 64-byte buffer? 0x40=64 bytes: indices 0..63; writing dest[v21] with v21=64 → writes byte 64 = OFF BY ONE). Actually the check `(v21 - 1) >= 0x3F` rejects v21-1 ≥ 63, i.e., accepts v21-1 ≤ 62 → v21 ≤ 63 → dest[v21] at index ≤63 → OK. Hmm: if v21-1 == 62 → v21=63 → dest[63]=0 within 64 bytes. If v21-1==63 → rejected. So max v21=63 → within bounds. Bounded. OK no overflow there.\n\nhttpRpmUpload basically uses memPool with sizes and content length bound; the multipart parse is bounded. Not obviously vulnerable.\n\nConclusion of the deep hunt: I could not identify a *defensible* memory-corruption or command-injection bug in httpd login / upload / DDNS / FS paths with clean evidence. The dynamic tools (qemu diff) show the harness cannot drive httpd request processing (both \"/\" and LoginRpm.htm reset; 0 diff), and function fuzz is broken (hook compile failure).\n\nPer the user's instruction: \"拿到 qemu 差分或 fuzz 结果后再决定是否入库\" — the qemu差分 results are in hand (two traces, 0 diff, trigger error), fuzz result is an infra error. Decision: NO findings to record (no real evidence of vulnerability). I must not fabricate.\n\nActually, hold on. Let me reconsider one more time whether there's legitimate observed evidence: The minidlnad trace is stuck; let me do one final poll and then wrap up with a clear report. Also double check fw","stream":true,"block":0}

