data: {"seq":407,"ts":"2026-08-22T16:19:23.987Z","text":"bpalogin login(): \n- `extract_valuestring(a1, v35, 0x16u, (char *)(a1 + 1174))` — copies server-supplied \"server list\" string into a1+1174. a1 is the config struct; buffer at a1+1174 for a list of server names separated by \" ,\". Then:\n```\nv18 = strcspn(v14, \" ,\"); v19 = v18;\nif (!v18) break;\nstrncpy(v34, v14, v18);     // v34[200] stack\nv34[v19] = 0;\nstrcpy((char *)(i + v16), v34);   // i = a1+1686, v16 = 20*cnt → server name table (20 bytes each)\n```\n`strncpy(v34, v14, v18)` with v18 up to... strcspn limit = end of string; if the server list string is longer than 200 (v34), strncpy copies all (no NUL), v34[v19]=0 writes beyond 200 → **stack overflow** if v19 >= 200! v18 = number of chars until delimiter. Wait strncpy(v34, v14, v18) — writes v18 bytes to v34[200]; if v18 >= 200 → overflow. Then `v34[v19]=0` (v19=v18). So a server-list token longer than ~200 bytes from UDP → stack overflow in login().\n\nBUT: what bounds extract_valuestring? It copies into (a1+1174) — need extract_valuestring to see. Also the first server token list: typically \"billing server list\" from login response field 0x16 — attacker (MITM auth server or the actual server) controls it. The struct a1: +1174 (list buffer) — how big? Then i=a1+1686: buffer from 1686 to 1174+... The tokens are copied into a1+1686+i*20 slots. `strcpy((char *)(i+v16), v34)` — 20-byte slots; if v34 > 20 bytes → overflow into the next slots/struct. And cnt = 12248 counter, slots until... interesting: token length is capped nowhere! v18 up to ~len of the server list string (could be e.g. 1000 bytes from UDP), so strncpy(v34, v14, v18) writes up to v18 into v34[200] → stack overflow (v34 is 200 bytes at sp+38).\n\nThis is in login() — reached from mainloop → when? login is the auth/pppd triggering function. Who calls login? Probably on configuration/connection event (startup), triggered after successful protocol negotiation? Actually the \"login\" here = the VoIP/BPA login transaction supported by bpalogin; it runs when the \"config\" says so or on button. The attacker controls the UDP *response* (auth server). The response field 0x16 (T_MSG... 22 = \"server list\"?) — attacker-controlled value → overflow crasher.\n\nHowever: is field 0x16 present in the *response* we can provoke? login() is called from... let me not dig too deep. The reachability: bpalogin receives UDP from the configured auth server; attacker on the same LAN could spoof the server (UDP). The parsing happens in login().\n\nThe overflow: v34[200] stack buffer overwritten with attacker data (up to full token length), then strcpy of that into the struct (heap/global). Gives stack overflow → RCE on MIPS (no canary; checksec of bpalogin unknown — fetch quickly).\n\nBut wait: extract_valuestring((a1+1174)) — the source max length? Let me check extract_valuestring bounds; the response field's data length (v4 = *(...+1512) is the transaction length; parse walks fields). If extract_valuestring is unbounded (no maxlen arg!) — signature extract_valuestring(a1, a2, a3, a4) — no length param → it copies the whole field into dest without bound → overflow of the field buffer a1+1174 (struct) too. Need to check bounds. Also v34[200] overflow is on stack — cleanest.\n\nLet me read extract_valuestring and mainloop + callers of login to confirm reachability, plus check bpalogin checksec (via a trace request? no — can't run without trace... checksec not exposed except in trace errors. Typically these uClibc binaries: no canary).\n\nGiven remaining budget, this is a reasonable final push: read extract_valuestring (search) and mainloop (0x401010).","stream":false,"block":0}

