ce — not possible for UDP with the HTTP harness). \n\nActually wait — I should double check the claim \"unbounded\": is there any outer length check? v13 = 16-bit field length from datagram (a2+...) — v13-4 bytes copied. No comparison against dest buffer size anywhere. The dests are: a1+1150 (nonce, protocol neg phase) and a1+1174 (server list), a1+12252 (server name). The config struct a1: v35 is a separate 1520+ byte buffer (start_transaction v35 = local array `_DWORD v35[380]` = 1520 bytes + 8 = 1528; v4 = *(+1512) = length). a1 = the config object (~13KB, malloc'd or BSS).\n\nWith default deployment, bpalogin might not even be enabled (BPA = broadband phone adapter; Archer C7 may include it for VoIP over WAN?). IN-002 lists it as UDP public input bind/recvfrom/sendto — it's in the identification as a listener. Since identification gate_1 says all are public inputs, bpalogin binds UDP and listens (receive_udp_transaction = recvfrom). Actually receive_udp_transaction does recvfrom on the socket — could be the *server side* (receiving incoming datagrams). Two directions: (1) incoming datagram → sub_402820/sub_402870 parse → handle (heartbeat/reply handling); (2) outgoing (login() connect). For the overflow in login(), the attacker responds to bpalogin's initiated session — the datagram *read* happens on","stream":true,"block":0}

