data: {"seq":473,"ts":"2026-08-22T16:19:57.086Z","text":"extract_valuestring: `memcpy(a4, v11, v13 - 4)` where v13 = the field's declared length (16-bit from the datagram). **UNBOUNDED memcpy into a4** — the destination buffer is supplied by caller (fixed struct offset or stack) with no size limit! In login():\n- extract_valuestring(a1, v35, 0x16u, (char *)(a1+1174)) — field 0x16 (server list, ≤ 65535 bytes per field!) into struct buffer at +1174.\n- extract_valuestring(a1, v35, 0xCu, (char *)(a1+1150)) — nonce into +1150.\n- extract_valuestring(a1, v35, 9u, (char *)(a1+12252)) — server name into +12252.\n\nThe dest offsets: native struct... a1+1150 nonce: field len from UDP; if nonce longer than struct space → overflow of the config struct (heap or global). And v33[16] (challenge send) etc.\n\nAlso, the field length (v13-4) is attacker-controlled (16-bit from datagram) — so memcpy(dest, src, up to 65531). a4 for nonce at a1+1150. What's the struct? a1 = config block (probably heap ~13KB since +12768 offsets used). The overflow from +1150 by >N bytes → corrupts adjacent struct fields / heap metadata → RCE on the MIPS device (no NX/canary per checksec pattern of these bins). And the \"server list\" at a1+1174 → overflow into the server-name table area (~12KB region) — corrupting a1+1686 slots and beyond (a1+12248 counters...).\n\nBut wait — is login() actually called with network-derived transaction? login() runs the *auth protocol client* — it connects to the auth server and processes its responses. The response datagram is network input (UDP from auth server). Who invokes login()? The call graph has no resolvable callers (maybe via function pointer table or from mainloop with a command). Let me read bpalogin main (0x40482c) and mainloop (0x401010) to establish the entry: does the *incoming UDP datagram* (receive_udp_transaction → sub_402870) dispatch commands to login/logout functions? If an incoming packet of type \"login request\" triggers login() — then the transaction v35 is built from the incoming datagram (server → client messages)! In login(), v35 = start_transaction(...) then receive_transaction(a1, v2, v35) fills v35 from the network — then extract_* reads fields from the received datagram. So yes: fields 0x16/0xC/0x9 come from the received protocol-negotiation/auth responses over UDP → attacker-controlled.\n\nSo the chain: UDP datagram → mainloop/sub_402870 dispatch → login() → receive_transaction (fills v35 from socket) → extract_valuestring(..., field, dest) → memcpy(dest, src, field_len-4) unbounded → overflow.\n\nThe PoC: craft a UDP packet to bpalogin that triggers T_MSG_PROTOCOL_NEG/LOGIN response... but login() itself initiates (connect + send). The attacker must *respond* to bpalogin's requests (MITM or spoofing the auth server IP — bpalogin connects to a config-specified server). So the attacker must be able to respond on the same 4-tuple — i.e., on-path (LAN MITM) or compromise the auth server. Reachability = requires on-path network position. bpalogin's auth server IP is in configuration (web-accessible? set via httpd settings for the VoIP line?). \n\nHmm, this is becoming a plausible finding but reachability is \"requires spoofing the configured auth server on the LAN\". For a router firmware, an attacker already on the LAN can spoof any IP... bpalogin listens via receive_udp_transaction on its own sockets (bind + connect). Since bpalogin *connects* to the server and reads *responses*, an on-LAN attacker can spoof packets from the server IP:port to the client's source port (which is what getsockname shows — kernel picks a port after bind(0)? they bind (a1+984) — maybe a fixed port). If fixed, easy spoof.\n\nOK — regardless, this is at least a *credible static finding*: unbounded memcpy of 16-bit length into fixed destination buffers in bpalogin's protocol field extraction (extract_valuestring), with login() as the processing context. This is defensible as static-only (observed reachability would need trace — not possible for UDP with the HTTP harness). \n\nActually wait — I should double check the claim \"unbounded\": is there any outer length check? v13 = 16-bit field length from datagram (a2+...) — v13-4 bytes copied. No comparison against dest buffer size anywhere. The dests are: a1+1150 (nonce, protocol neg phase) and a1+1174 (server list), a1+12252 (server name). The config struct a1: v35 is a separate 1520+ byte buffer (start_transaction v35 = local array `_DWORD v35[380]` = 1520 bytes + 8 = 1528; v4 = *(+1512) = length). a1 = the config object (~13KB, malloc'd or BSS).\n\nWith default deployment, bpalogin might not even be enabled (BPA = broadband phone adapter; Archer C7 may include it for VoIP over WAN?). IN-002 lists it as UDP public input bind/recvfrom/sendto — it's in the identification as a listener. Since identification gate_1 says all are public inputs, bpalogin binds UDP and listens (receive_udp_transaction = recvfrom). Actually receive_udp_transaction does recvfrom on the socket — could be the *server side* (receiving incoming datagrams). Two directions: (1) incoming datagram → sub_402820/sub_402870 parse → handle (heartbeat/reply handling); (2) outgoing (login() connect). For the overflow in login(), the attacker responds to bpalogin's initiated session — the datagram *read* happens on the *connected* socket. On-path spoof required.\n\nGiven uncertainty about bpalogin's actual deployment on this device (it might never run unless configured), I'll record this as **static-only, medium severity, confidence ~0.5**, as a defensible documented candidate with call chain and PoC (spoofed UDP response). This satisfies \"record_finding\" with static-only cap 0.7. And it's honestly described (not over-claimed).\n\nWait, but I should verify the trigger: which received field triggers? In login(), extract_valuestring dests:\n- field 0x18 (24) → (a1+1020) — protocol neg: \"server name\"?\n- field 0xC (12) → nonce (a1+1150) — in auth phase (after T_MSG_AUTH_RESP).\n- field 0x16 (22) → server list (a1+1174)\n- field 9 → (a1+12252) server name?? Actually that one is after login success (from T_MSG_LOGIN_RESP).\n\n`if ( !extract_valuestring(a1, (int)v35, 0xCu, (char *)(a1 + 1150)) )` — the nonce from auth response. A nonce field > (struct size - 1150) → overflow. Struct size: offsets up to +12772 used → struct ~13KB (a1 allocated 13056?). Nonce would need ~12KB to smash far; but even a few hundred bytes overflow past the intended nonce buffer corrupts the server list table and counters.\n\nAlright, record it. Also the earlier finding candidates: I've decided no other findings.\n\nHmm, wait. Let me be careful about \"extract_valuestring memcpy(v13-4)\" — v13 includes 4-byte header (2 header + 2 len?). memcpy(a4, v11, v13-4) — v11 points at value start. v13 = len field. So it copies len-4 bytes. Attacker sets len field = huge (up to 65535). Fine.\n\nBut one more check: field 0x16's processing loop in login():\n```\nv14 = (a1+1174);  // server list\nextract_valuestring(a1, v35, 0x16u, (char *)(a1 + 1174));\nextract_valuestring(a1, v35, 9u, (char *)(a1 + 12252));\n...\nstrncpy(v34, v14, v18); ... strcpy((char *)(i + v16), v34);  // i=a1+1686\n```\nThe server list at +1174 → parse into tokens → copied into 20-byte slots at +1686 with **unbounded strcpy** (v34 token can be up to... strcspn to delimiter; token could be huge (from the overflowed/attacker list). strcpy(dest_slot, v34) — slot is 20 bytes, token up to 198+ bytes → overflow of the slot table (20*cnt). This is a 2nd overflow in the same chain","stream":true,"block":0}

