data: {"seq":19,"ts":"2026-08-22T16:26:11.729Z","text":"The user wants me to record the confirmed entry-point vulnerabilities with reproducible conclusions. Let me carefully assess what is confirmed:\n\n1. **bpalogin login() server-list stack overflow** — let me finish verifying:\n   - `extract_valuestring(..., a4)` does `memcpy(a4, v11, v13-4)` UNBOUNDED (v13 = field length from wire, 16-bit up to 65535), then `a4[v13-4]=0`. a4 points to struct offsets like a1+1174, a1+1150, a1+1020, a1+12252.\n   - In login(): `extract_valuestring(a1, v35, 0x16u, (char *)(a1 + 1174))` — copies wire string (field 0x16 length v13 from packet!) into a1+1174. The struct a1 sizes: a1+1174... what's the buffer size at a1+1174? Following: token parse loop uses v14 = a1+1174, tokens copied via strncpy(v34[200], v14, v18) stack → v34 overflow if token ≥ 200 bytes; also strcpy into a1+1686+20*cnt.\n   - BUT reachability: who calls login()? Call graph shows no callers (gap). bpalogin main: the interface to login likely via \"authentication type\" from config; the actual flow: main () [0x40482c] → runs mainloop → on program start, performs login (pppd auth)? bpalogin = \"Broadband Phone/PPP logon\" client: it logs in to the ISP's auth server over UDP and then launches pppd. So the login transaction is the *core* function, run at startup and on renegotiation. The auth server (remote UDP endpoint, attacker-spoofable on same network) sends the Login Response (type 5) with fields incl. 0x16 = \"server list\" → overflow.\n   - Is this the \"public input\"? bpalogin is IN-002 UDP public (per identification). Port unknown — bpalogin connects OUT to the auth server port; the \"input\" is the response datagram.\n   \n   So this is a defensible static finding: **stack buffer overflow in bpalogin login() server-list parsing** (CWE-121/787), reachable from a UDP auth-server response (MITM/spoofable), no length limit on token (strncpy v18 up to full token length into v34[200]).\n\n   Wait, one more check: `v18 = strcspn(v14, \" ,\")` — token length bounded by the string until space/comma. The string came from extract_valuestring into a1+1174 — v13-4 bytes (16-bit max 65535!) copied into a1+1174 — that itself may overflow a1+1174's struct buffer (need size). And then strncpy(v34, v14, v18) — if v18 ≥ 200 → v34 overflow (real stack smashing). Also `strcpy((char*)(i+v16), v34)` where slots are 20 bytes → slot overflow too (writes v34 token > 20 bytes into subsequent slots, plus beyond table end into other struct members).\n\n   The primary, cleanest bug: v34[200] stack overflow via arbitrarily long server-list token — BUT wait, is it bounded by the earlier overflow target a1+1174? Suppose a1+1174 buffer is, say, 512 bytes (need to verify struct). The token could still exceed 200 (e.g. 400-byte token with no delimiter) → strncpy overflows v34. Even if the a1+1174 copy were bounded by struct size, the parse loop takes delimiters; a 512-byte list with one 300-byte token > 200 → overflow. So bug stands as long as the field can be ≥ 200 bytes and doesn't hit an earlier OOB; extract_valuestring has NO length param — unbounded memcpy into a1+1174 from","stream":true,"block":0}

