 `v18 = strcspn(v14, \" ,\")` — token length bounded by the string until space/comma. The string came from extract_valuestring into a1+1174 — v13-4 bytes (16-bit max 65535!) copied into a1+1174 — that itself may overflow a1+1174's struct buffer (need size). And then strncpy(v34, v14, v18) — if v18 ≥ 200 → v34 overflow (real stack smashing). Also `strcpy((char*)(i+v16), v34)` where slots are 20 bytes → slot overflow too (writes v34 token > 20 bytes into subsequent slots, plus beyond table end into other struct members).\n\n   The primary, cleanest bug: v34[200] stack overflow via arbitrarily long server-list token — BUT wait, is it bounded by the earlier overflow target a1+1174? Suppose a1+1174 buffer is, say, 512 bytes (need to verify struct). The token could still exceed 200 (e.g. 400-byte token with no delimiter) → strncpy overflows v34. Even if the a1+1174 copy were bounded by struct size, the parse loop takes delimiters; a 512-byte list with one 300-byte token > 200 → overflow. So bug stands as long as the field can be ≥ 200 bytes and doesn't hit an earlier OOB; extract_valuestring has NO length param — unbounded memcpy into a1+1174 from a 16-bit length (up to 65535) — that's even bigger (heap/global struct overflow of server-list buffer). Both are real overflows;","stream":true,"block":0}

