 `v18 = strcspn(v14, \" ,\")` — token length bounded by the string until space/comma. The string came from extract_valuestring into a1+1174 — v13-4 bytes (16-bit max 65535!) copied into a1+1174 — that itself may overflow a1+1174's struct buffer (need size). And then strncpy(v34, v14, v18) — if v18 ≥ 200 → v34 overflow (real stack smashing). Also `strcpy((char*)(i+v16), v34)` where slots are 20 bytes → slot overflow too (writes v34 token > 20 bytes into subsequent slots, plus beyond table end into other struct members).\n\n   The primary, cleanest bug: v34[200] stack overflow via arbitrarily long server-list token — BUT wait, is it bounded by the earlier overflow target a1+1174? Suppose a1+1174 buffer is, say, 512 bytes (need to verify struct). The token could still exceed 200 (e.g. 400-byte token with no delimiter) → strncpy overflows v34. Even if the a1+1174 copy were bounded by struct size, the parse loop takes delimiters; a 512-byte list with one 300-byte token > 200 → overflow. So bug stands as long as the field can be ≥ 200 bytes and doesn't hit an earlier OOB; extract_valuestring has NO length param — unbounded memcpy into a1+1174 from a 16-bit length (up to 65535) — that's even bigger (heap/global struct overflow of server-list buffer). Both are real overflows; the stack one (v34) is the exploitable one.\n\n   Reachability chain: main (0x40482c) → mainloop (0x401010) → login (0x401ab8) [on auth request] → receive_transaction → extract_valuestring(field 0x16) → strcspn/strncpy → stack overflow.\n\n   Hmm, but \"login\" callers = empty in call graph. Let me verify mainloop/main dispatch quickly to confirm login() reachable — read mainloop (0x401010) and main (0x40482c) brief. This is important to claim reachability honestly. bpalogin on Archer C7 — is it even started? The rcS didn't show bpalogin. It could be started by uclited when VoIP config... It's in identification as an input so the pipeline treats it public. For a \"static-only\" finding, I can state the chain with a caveat that login is invoked from mainloop based on protocol state (need evidence).\n\n   Let me read mainloop/main to confirm.\n\n","stream":true,"block":0}

