data: {"seq":95,"ts":"2026-08-22T16:26:44.607Z","text":"Confirmed: mainloop() calls login(a1) directly (`if ( !login(a1) )`). And main() calls mainloop(). So chain: main (0x40482c) → mainloop (0x401010) → login (0x401ab8) → receive_transaction → extract_valuestring(field 0x16, dst=a1+1174) → strcspn/strncpy(v34[200]) → stack overflow w/ >200-byte token.\n\nbpalogin is IN-002 UDP public input. The auth server is remote (network attacker in path; UDP spoofable). The crafted flow: bpalogin sends login transaction (type 1 protocol neg), expects T_MSG_PROTOCOL_NEG_RESP (2), then sends auth (3), expects response (9) — extract_valuestring(a1+1150 nonce, a1+1020), then computes digest, sends login (4), receives T_MSG_LOGIN_RESP (5) → then extracts:\n```\nextract_valueINT2(a1, v35, 0xAu, (a1+1168));      // status code\nextract_valueINT2(...0x10u, (a1+1170));\nextract_valueINT2(...0x11u, (a1+1172));\nextract_valuestring(a1, v35, 0x16u, (char *)(a1 + 1174));   // server list — OVERFLOW target\nextract_valuestring(a1, v35, 9u, (char *)(a1 + 12252));\n```\n\nSo the overflow triggers on the **Login Response** datagram with field 0x16 (22) containing a long value. The transaction format: field records: 2-byte type, 2-byte len, data... The response must pass earlier checks (type 5, status code 0/5... wait status extraction happens BEFORE 0x16: status in a1+1168 from field 0x0A. The field 0x16 extraction happens at LABEL_37 AFTER status checks — status must be 0 (or 5? branch v13==5 disallowed... statuses: 0 = success). So the attacker crafts T_MSG_LOGIN_RESP (5) with status code 0, then field 0x16 = long string.\n\nAlso note the earlier `extract_valuestring(a1, v35, 0xCu, (char *)(a1 + 1150))` (nonce) in the AUTH_RESP (type 9) path — a1+1150 buffer; a 16-bit length up to 65535 → overflow of nonce buffer too. Both are unbounded copies from wire into struct buffers, plus the v34 stack overflow.\n\nBut wait — the auth path requires the attacker to respond to the client's auth request with valid digest computation? Look: after AUTH_RESP (9): nonce extracted, `if (*(a1+1148)==1) { hash password }`, then the client computes its own response via add_field_data (digest) and sends T_MSG_LOGIN (4); the server must reply T_MSG_LOGIN_RESP (5). So attacker needs to send: PROTOCOL_NEG_RESP(2) → AUTH_RESP(9) with nonce/hashmethod → LOGIN_RESP(5) with status 0 + long field 0x16. The digest check: does bpalogin verify the server's HMAC? KAME bpalogin... this is \"bpalogin\" (Juniper BPA/iPhone?). In the decomp, I don't see any server-authentication verification of the login response — the client just checks type and status. The earlier protocol neg response check: type 2, protocol 1... no crypto verification seen (the digest is client→server only). So a MITM/spoofed UDP server reply works.\n\nReachability grade: static-only (no dynamic verify possible for UDP via this harness). Confidence: the bug is real in code (unbounded copies), reachability plausible but needs the login flow; I'll cap confidence ~0.6 (memory-corruption without checksec evidence ≤0.6 per rules).\n\nChecksec for bpalogin: unknown (CANARY false likely — but I shouldn't assert; the record backend may skip). Actually earlier httpd/minidlnad checksec showed canary:false/nx:false/pie:false. bpalogin is same toolchain (uClibc, MIPS) → very likely no stack canary; but I can't verify via tool (no checksec output for bpalogin unless trace runs — trace can't run bpalogin? It could run qemu baseline! Let me try a trace on bpalogin to (a) obtain checksec evidence, (b) dynamic baseline. bpalogin will run, bind UDP socket, then call login → tries to connect to auth server... under qemu-user network? UDP connect will fail/timeout; the run would hang similar to minidlnad. Not worth it. I'll record static-only with confidence 0.6.\n\nActually wait — maybe checksec data is in the job's manifest. The traces show checksec for binaries I traced. There might be a manifest.json in the extracted root. Let me check via fw_browse_firmware manifest.json? The browse root is the job root which contains \"manifest.json\". Let me read it (json text allowed). It might contain per-binary checksec/arch info for all binaries incl. md5 08b086 (xl2tpd) etc. Quick read.\n\nAlso the CVE class/cwe: CWE-121 (stack-based buffer overflow) for v34[200]; the overflow is stack. vuln_class \"stack overflow\"? must be SINGLE class without '/'. Use \"stack-overflow\"? Server rule: values containing / etc rejected; \"stack overflow\" with space fine. I'll use \"stack_overflow\"... hmm safer to use \"overflow\" or \"buffer-overflow\"? The rule says vuln_class single class; no combos. Common accepted: \"buffer overflow\"? contains space — allowed (no / anyway). I'll use \"stack overflow\" as vuln_class? To be safe use \"buffer overflow\".\n\nSeverity: high (RCE on router, though requires network position on the auth-server path; bpalogin runs as root? likely). medium-high → use \"high\"? It's a UDP-spoofable stack overflow in a root daemon → high.\n\nReachability: static-only (no trace_id). Confidence: 0.6 (cap 0.7, and memory-corruption without checksec ≤0.6 — I have no checksec evidence for bpalogin → 0.6).\n\ncall_chain: main(0x40482c)→mainloop(0x401010)→login(0x401ab8)→extract_valuestring(0x402bb8)→strncpy(v34, v14, v18) / v34[v19]=0. Include addresses.\n\nPoC: describe UDP login response datagram: fields encoded big-endian u16 type/u16 len: Transaction type 5 (T_MSG_LOGIN_RESP), fields: 0x0A len2 value=0 (status ok), 0x16 len=N value = 300+ 'A's. Also need protocol-neg (type 2) & auth-resp (type 9) first with nonce field 0x0C. Provide python","stream":true,"block":0}

