data: {"seq":170,"ts":"2026-08-22T16:31:07.618Z","text":"Manifest has checksec for all binaries. I only need bpalogin's entry — but the manifest is 54KB and truncated listing. Let me note that all binaries so far show canary=false. bpalogin is presumably also canary=false (same toolchain). I can attempt to read the specific part of manifest? The browse read capped at 64KB and it's a single JSON — I can't grep. Let me just check the probability — the pattern is consistent (all non-libc binaries: canary false, nx false). bpalogin md5 f8a1d1f6... — high confidence canary=false. I'll write evidence \"checksec(manifest): canary=false (工具链一致)\" — but to be precise, don't over-assert; confidence 0.6 anyway.\n\nNow write the PoC script in the workspace. Then record the finding.\n\nPoC design (bpalogin protocol, transaction wire format):\n- Transactions: header = 8 bytes: 2-byte type? Let me reconstruct from extract functions: start_transaction(v35, type, id)... v35 = buffer; parse: first 8 bytes header: field walk starts at a2+8 with (u16 type at +8..+9, u16 len at +10..+11), value follows at +12... Wait in extract_valueINT2:\n```\nv6 = a2+8;\nif (a3 != (u16)(v6[1] + (v6[0]<<8))) {   // compare field type\n   v7 = (u16)(v6[3] + (v6[2]<<8));        // field len\n   v8 = 8; v6 += v7 → next field header at a2+8+v7\n   ...\n   v9 = v6 + 4;   // value = after 4-byte header\n...\nv9 = a2 + 12;   // first field value\n```\nSo header per field: 2B type, 2B len, then len bytes value. First field at offset 8; value at offset 12 (for first field). v4 = total transaction length at (a2+1512)?? Odd — *(_DWORD *)(a2 + 1512) is the transaction length stored in the buffer after receive (a2 = v35 = 380 dwords = 1520 bytes; +1512 = last dword = length). \n\nIn extract_valuestring: v13 = field len (u16); memcpy(a4, v11, v13-4); a4[v13-4]=0. Note v11 = value start (a2+12 for first field), so the field len includes the 4-byte header? \"v13-4\" suggests len counts type+len+value. So field (type=0x16, len=L) → value bytes = L-4.\n\nSo Login Response (type 5) transaction layout:\n- bytes 0..7: transaction header (type=5 in first 2 bytes? start_transaction(v35, 5, id) — likely writes type at +0). Then field #1: type=0x0A (10), len=0x0006, value = u16 status 0 (2 bytes) + ...? extract_valueINT2 reads at v9 (value) 2 bytes. So value size = len-4 = 2 → len = 6.\n- field: type=0x16 (22), len = 4 + N, value = 'A'*N with N ≥ 200 (e.g., 512).\n\nThen login()'s parse: v14 = a1+1174 (from extract_valuestring), token loop: strncpy(v34, v14, v18) → v34 overflow.\n\nAlso the earlier fields (0x10, 0x11) must exist (u16 values) — they're optional per extract (returns 0 if missing, no failure). Actually extract_valueINT2 assigns via *a4 only if found; if missing, *a4 unchanged (stack garbage → v17...). Fine.\n\nReachability requirement: bpalogin connected to auth server already got PROTOCOL_NEG_RESP(2) and AUTH_RESP(9). We need to respond as the server on the UDP port bpalogin bound for auth (a1+800 port = getsockname local port? \"Listening on port %d\" — from *(_WORD *)addr.sa_data after getsockname on the UDP socket... wait the socket is bound to (a1+984) with port (a1+986) = v3 = config remote auth port? Actually bind uses a1+984 (sin family=2, port=v3 at +986) — hmm the local port. Then connects to auth server at a1+804/806. The \"external input\" for spoofing is the server's replies arriving on this socket.\n\nFor the PoC eth: an attacker on the LAN-facing path spoofs the auth server's IP:port (UDP) or is a MITM; or configures a rogue auth server address (bpalogin config port field at a1+210/50... config from command line: main parses argv: -host etc.). Simplest PoC: run a rogue UDP responder on the configured auth server port that answers the 3 transactions, and send LOGIN_RESP with the long field 0x16.\n\nWrite the PoC as a python script in workspace. Then record_finding with:\n- title: bpalogin login() 登录响应服务器列表解析栈缓冲区溢出\n- severity: high\n- vuln_class: buffer overflow (single class, no /) — allowed? \"buffer overflow\" has space only. OK.\n- cwe: CWE-121\n- binary_md5: f8a1d1f62eef2e1e73cbbeadd5d51900\n- binary_path: firmware/binwalk_extracted/firmware.extracted/100000/squashfs-root/usr/sbin/bpalogin\n- summary (Chinese), evidence (addresses), confidence 0.6, reachability static-only, call_chain with → inclThe manifest confirms the toolchain: all application binaries (arpping, minidlnad, etc.) have canary:false, nx:false, pie:false, relro:none — this uClibc MIPS toolchain builds no mitigations. bpalogin is in the same toolchain, so canary=false/nx=false is a very safe inference; but to be rigorous I should confirm bpalogin is in the manifest. The manifest is 54KB; the trace of bpalogin could also give checksec. But the manifest excerpt shows the pattern. Let me confirm bpalogin entry in the manifest instead of reading the whole 54KB (browse caps at 64KB, so full read works but response is huge). Better: I can verify checksec empirically by running a quick trace on bpalogin? The trace would hang (daemon). Hmm, but the failed-trace output includes checksec. Actually a trace that fails fast would work: run bpalogin with invalid argv (e.g., argv0 \"bpalogin\" with a nonexistent config) so it exits quickly → checksec in the failed result. But that wastes trace slot (minidlnad possibly still stuck). The manifest likely includes bpalogin (usr/s","stream":true,"block":0}

