data: {"seq":42,"ts":"2026-08-22T16:40:49.086Z","text":"Good news: the minidlnad stuck trace was killed by orchestrator restart (4cfb0cac0b5c failed) — trace slot is FREE now. Also someone ran another httpd trace (93b94696d1f5) — same 0-diff reset result.\n\nTrace budget: used 4 completed (3 httpd ok-empty, 1 minidlnad failed) → 8 more available.\n\nNow, continue the hunt. High-value: dnsproxy (UDP DNS parser, IN-005 public). DNS packet parsing in custom \"dnsproxy\" is often buggy (recursion, pointer handling). Let me examine dnsproxy more closely:\n- dns_read_packet (recv)\n- dns_decode_request (0x403238) — strncpy bounds look OK, but let me see the whole picture: the packet layout: a1 = buf (4096), qname at a1+324, answer parsing... sub_4028D8, sub_402E18 function calls. Sinks: memunsafe.\n- Also dnsproxy has conf_cmdparse (from config file).\n\nAlso there's bpalogin type overflow I recorded. Let me also confirm the dhcp6c side: dhcp6c client (ae35a5...) parses server messages in dhcp6_get_options @0x40bb8c — same codebase as dhcp6s. Also dhcp6c has other parsing: receive_advertise etc. But dhcp6c as a \"public input\" is odd (it's a client).\n\nPriorities now (static + dynamic):\n1. dnsproxy full parse chain — find unbounded memcpy with DNS name length.\n2. dhcp6s response builder sub_405170 (sends to client) — any fixed-buffer snprintf with attacker option data? Actually the *client* controls the options in request; server builds reply options from config mostly, plus echoes? Let me check sub_405170 size-limited.\n3. uclited HTTP mini server (StartMiniServer) — big target.\n\nBut the user says \"确认一个漏洞就入库\" — one confirmed vuln per iteration. bpalogin is recorded. Now continue to find another confirmed one.\n\nGiven dynamic harness limits (HTTP GET only; UDP not driveable), the achievable \"confirmation\" is static-only again (like bpalogin) unless we find an HTTP-reachable bug in httpd/uclited/minidlnad... httpd request processing can't be driven by the harness. uclited's StartMiniServer might bind HTTP on a port and the harness GET could drive it — uclited is a big TCP daemon that runs the \"mini server\" (web server on port 80?) plus DNS queries. uclited argv: started as \"/usr/bin/uclited\" (from rcS!). Its mini server is probably the main HTTP landing zone. If harness GET drives uclited's HTTP loop → diff! That would be real dynamic verification.\n\nWait — actually there were two HTTP things: httpd (port 80) and uclited also running. Historically, uclited in TP-Link runs the \"uc\" IPC over a TCP socket and may host a web UI on port 80 too... unclear. Let me test uclited with a trace (argv [] port 80 or 0?). Port unknown; try 80.\n\nBut first, could the httpd reset issue be avoided by giving httpd the \"-f\"? no. Hmm.\n\nLet me do TWO things:\n1. Request a trace for uclited (md5 d61e67ed20163b7fee8bd3c606fb0331), argv [], port 80, \"/\" — see if it differs (dynamic).\n2. Meanwhile statically scan dnsproxy parse functions (sub_4028D8/sub_402E18 + dns_read_packet) for unbounded copies; and uclited StartMiniServer handler.\n\nNote: traces are serialized — the uclited trace will block other traces while running (~10s each) — fine.\n\nLet me fire trace + static reads.","stream":true,"block":0}

