data: {"seq":57,"ts":"2026-08-22T16:40:54.779Z","text":"sub_4028D8 in dnsproxy: decodes a DNS name (labels) into a buffer a1. **No bounds check on v7** — it copies label bytes and adds dots until the whole name is consumed. The caller must bound a1. Let me see the caller (dns_decode_request 0x403238 full) to see buffer size vs. input size. dns_decode_request brief showed strncpy((char *)(a1 + 255), (a1+324), 0x2Eu) and strncpy((char*)a1, (a1+324), 0xFFu) — a1 is a 4096 buffer. Where's sub_4028D8 called from? Let me read dns_decode_request full and find callers of sub_4028D8 (query name decode → buffer). DNS names can be up to 255 bytes; the decode expands to a1+... if the destination buffer is small (e.g., a1+0..255? a1+255..?) the output with dots could overflow.\n\nCareful: The name decode output adds a '.' separator (v7++ per label). If name is 255 bytes (labels), output = 255 bytes + up to 127 dots ≥ 255 → overflow of any 255-byte dest. Where's the dest? Let me read dns_decode_request full + callers of sub_4028D8","stream":true,"block":0}

