≤63, total ≤255 per RFC — but the decoder trusts the wire: label length byte v6 = up to 255! `v6 = *(*a2)++` — label length is a byte 0..255, then copies v6 bytes. Total name across labels could be bigger than 255 if malformed (e.g., 5 labels × 255 = 1275 bytes + dots) → v7 up to 1275+5 → still < 4096. No overflow of a1. OK safe.\n\nBUT wait — the loop `while (**a2)` stops at a zero byte; for a malformed name with length-byte > remaining packet bytes, it reads beyond the packet into the buffer (out-of-bounds *read* within the 4096 recv buffer — mild). No write overflow. Skip","stream":true,"block":0}

