data: {"seq":146,"ts":"2026-08-22T16:47:27.087Z","text":"Findings from this batch:\n\n1. radartool (sbin/radartool, md5 2c508f48...) — main with MANY strcpy of `*argv` (argv[0] actually! `strncpy(dest, *argv, 0x10)`, then `strcpy(v55, dest)`... wait line 78: dest = *argv — argv[0] (program name)!? It copies *argv (hmm) into dest[16]? `strncpy((char *)dest, *argv, 0x10u)` then strcpy(v55, dest) etc. dest here probably = interface name from argv[1]... brief shows \"*argv\" — that's argv[0], weird; more likely Hex-Rays shows *argv because dest was assigned earlier (dest = \"...\"?). The dangerous_calls list: strcpy(v55..v37, dest) repeatedly — dest = argv[1]?? radartool is ath radar tool (not public; sbin tool, probably local). It's network_facing (ioctl/socket to wifi0). Not a public input (not in identification). Skip.\n\n2. dhcp6ctl (usr/sbin/dhcp6ctl, md5 0053d7fc...) — a CLI control tool for dhcp6c (KAME). main → getopt → connect to control socket → sub_4010F0/sub_400C40 (memcpy sinks). dhcp6ctl is a *client* tool (not public input; used via CLI root). Skip.\n\n3. uclited trace: baseline 81 functions (much more than httpd's 30! uclited runs deeper under qemu), but trigger → **Connection refused** (port 80 not bound by uclited; httpd owns 80). So uclited doesn't listen on 80 with no args; needs its config (port from nvram/tp_usrconf). The mini server may bind a random port. Hmm. uclited argv: rcS starts \"/usr/bin/uclited\" — no port. Its server might listen on UDP port 1900 (SSDP) and a config-specified HTTP port. Dynamic GET won't drive it without config.\n\nNew idea for dynamic: uclited might read config from /tmp/etc/... but under qemu there's no nvram. Can't drive.\n\nOK. Given the harness's constraints, dynamic differential GET coverage works ONLY if: target binds an HTTP port under qemu-user with no external config, and request processing happens in the same process (not threads it can't follow). So far httpd fails (reset), minidlnad hangs, uclited doesn't bind 80.\n\nWhat about minidlnad with argv config? The earlier minidlnad failure was \"unsafe library search path\" (ELF RPATH containing build dir) — that's a qemu-user loader rejection. It could be worked around if the trace runner strips RPATH or sets QEMU_... — not under our control. Retry minidlnad maybe? It failed the same way twice (8519f7adc93d). The third attempt (4cfb0cac0b5c) ran (started), so the RPATH issue was fixed by orchestrator restart; but then it hung and was killed. minidlnad hangs because... it forks a scanner child and maybe the parent continues but no HTTP? Actually maybe it DID bind 8200 but the harness GET waits; the run \"hung\" 211s then killed. Timing: it hung at startup... Possibly because minidlnad without config file tries realpath(\"/tmp/mediaserver/cache\")→ make_dir — should work on the sandbox rootfs (writable?). It might block waiting for interface addresses (getifaddr on nonexistent ifaces) — n_lan_addr=0 → binds 0.0.0.0:8200. Then http server thread starts and the fork scanner...\n\nLet me try minidlnad once more with argv [\"-d\", \"-f\", \"/tmp/minidlna.conf\"]? -f = config file. We'd need a config file present in the sandbox — can't create. Default /etc/minidlna.conf — the extracted firmware root has /etc/minidlna.conf? Earlier main used name=\"/etc/minidlna.conf\". Let me check if file exists in the extraction (browse). If yes, minidlnad with default config might actually run and bind 8200. Also add \"-d\" (debug, stays foreground? no — minidlna main doesn't daemonize; forks scanner only).\n\nLet me check /etc/minidlna.conf exists.\n\nActually — hold on. Reconsider budget. Remaining traces ~8. Focus: get ONE confirmed vuln. The best remaining static candidates needing verification:\n\nA. The **dhcp6s relay-forward path** in sub_4065B4 — I only read half (truncated). The relay handling builds a linked list of relay-info (malloc 0x3C), recursion into encapsulated messages. Also `sub_405170` (server response builder). The most promising thing in dhcp6s: `dhcp6_vbuf_copy` and the auth option (case 11) `a3[53] = v7-31` etc. Also `setStatus` / status message. And `sub_402E7C` (auth init, \"credential compare\") reads... Let me read sub_405170 + dhcp6_vbuf_copy + sub_402E7C (in the P0 binary). One of these likely contains a memcpy with attacker length.\n\nB. **dnsproxy** recv path (dns_read_packet) + query forward writing — check buffer usage/limits.\n\nC. **athadhoc** (UDP, IN-001) — small binary (76 funcs); parse recvmsg payload. Likely has fixed-buffer ops. Quick scan of its dispatcher (sub_4033A0/sub_400AB0) and recv path for memcpy of datagram.\n\nD. **uclited** — huge; the config/network handling: `setIfNameDhcpStaticRoute`, `executeDhcpStaticRoute`, `saveDhcpStaticRoute` (0x4400ec-44023c) — these write route table via system()? uclited dispatcher list included \"setIfNameDhcpStaticRoute @ 0x43fffc\" etc. In TP-Link, static route settings come from the web (httpd IPC → uclited applies with `ip route add ...` via system). If uclited builds a shell command from web-provided dest/netmask/gw → command injection! That's a strong candidate: uclited \"ExecuteStaticRouteTable\" @0x43fd18. Let me read that. The web sends route via \"uc\" IPC (msg queue/共享内存) — the \"source\" is network (web), and uclited's chain: ipc recv → executeDhcpStaticRoute → snprintf(\"route add ...\") → system. Let me read ExecuteStaticRouteTable (0x43fd18), setDhcpStaticRoute (0x44023c), saveDhcpStaticRoute (0x4400ec), executeDhcpStaticRoute (0x4401ac).\n\nLet me read those uclited functions + check /etc/minidlna.conf presence.","stream":true,"block":0}

