data: {"seq":192,"ts":"2026-08-22T16:47:46.123Z","text":"sub_43F5C0 is a real command execution sink:\n```\nsprintf(v3, \"route add -net %s netmask %s gw %s dev %s\", v4, v5, v6, LanBridgeName);\nsystem(v3);\n```\n- v4 = IP addr string of route dest & mask (from swIpAddr2Str of numeric DWORDs)\n- v5 = netmask string\n- v6 = gateway string\n- LanBridgeName = ifname from a1+16 (route entry's ifname field!) or getLanBridgeName()/getWanIfName()\n\nThe ifname at a1+16 is attacker-supplied if the web lets you set the interface name/device for a static route. In TP-Link web UI \"Static Route\" config: fields = destination, subnet mask, gateway, interface (select box: LAN/WAN/... typically from a fixed dropdown). If the interface is a select, values are fixed strings; but many TP-Link firmware versions allow editing — in the older UI, \"接口\" was a select with \"LAN\"/\"WAN\"; the value passed through IPC might be an index→name lookup. If it's an arbitrary string from web (e.g., custom ifName), command injection via `;` in the ifname → system(RCE). Similarly gateway/ip are numeric (swIpAddr2Str from in_addr) → not injectable.\n\nBUT the reachability of this from network input: uclited receives config via IPC from httpd after login. It's the same \"web→system\" class. Which httpd function calls the equivalent \"set\" path? In httpd there are swSetStaticRouteCfg? Hmm — actually the flow: httpd writes route config to uclited via ucMsg (system message) — uclited's uc interface: \"ucStaticRouteCfg\" (referenced in ExecuteStaticRouteTable: ucStaticRouteCfg_getSize()). The data origin: web form → httpd (auth) → IPC → uclited → ExecuteStaticRouteTable.\n\nCommand injection on ifname (a1+16): where does a1+16 come from? Route table entry struct: a1: [0]=dest ip, [4]=netmask, [8]=gateway, [12]=flag?, [16]=ifname string. The ifname field is a string within the route entry that came from the web/IPC buffer. If the web sends \"ifname\" free-form... In TP-Link's newer UI there IS a \"接口\" text field? Not sure. This is the classic CVE family: TP-Link 命令注入 in uclited via 静态路由接口名 (e.g., CVE-2019-17622? no that was a different one...). Actually yes: **CVE-2018-14335**?? Hmm, TP-Link Archer series \"command injection in uclited via 'Interface' field of the static route page\" — the well-known one is CVE-2018-... for TL-WR841N and Archer via \"interface name\" — I recall \"TEW-…\". Let me not rely on CVEs; the code shows sprintf+system with ifname embedded. If ifname is injectable → vuln.\n\nLet me find where route entries are filled from IPC: search functions with \"StaticRoute\" in uclited: we have getStaticRouteTblSize, ExecuteStaticRouteTable, setIfNameDhcpStaticRoute, saveDhcpStaticRoute (0x4400ec), executeDhcpStaticRoute, flushDhcpStaticRoute, setDhcpStaticRoute. And in httpd side — the web page StaticRouteTableRpm.htm / VirtualServer etc. handlers call uc* IPC sets. The static route config path: httpd reads form fields incl. \"interface\"/\"destip\"/\"netmask\"/\"gateway\" → builds a route entry → sends to uclited (via shared mem \"sysvar\"?) . Under the hood it's \"sendMsgToHwNat\" / ucStaticRouteCfg... \n\nFor evidence-based confirmation, I need to trace the ifname value from the web input to a1+16. Let me look at httpd side route config functions: search \"StaticRoute\" in httpd; and \"swSetStaticRoute\"? Let me search both binaries.\n\nAlso check saveDhcpStaticRoute (0x4400ec) & setIfNameDhcpStatic","stream":true,"block":0}

event: thinking
data: {"seq":193,"ts":"2026-08-22T16:47:46.481Z","text":"sub_43F5C0 is a real command execution sink:\n```\nsprintf(v3, \"route add -net %s netmask %s gw %s dev %s\", v4, v5, v6, LanBridgeName);\nsystem(v3);\n```\n- v4 = IP addr string of route dest & mask (from swIpAddr2Str of numeric DWORDs)\n- v5 = netmask string\n- v6 = gateway string\n- LanBridgeName = ifname from a1+16 (route entry's ifname field!) or getLanBridgeName()/getWanIfName()\n\nThe ifname at a1+16 is attacker-supplied if the web lets you set the interface name/device for a static route. In TP-Link web UI \"Static Route\" config: fields = destination, subnet mask, gateway, interface (select box: LAN/WAN/... typically from a fixed dropdown). If the interface is a select, values are fixed strings; but many TP-Link firmware versions allow editing — in the older UI, \"接口\" was a select with \"LAN\"/\"WAN\"; the value passed through IPC might be an index→name lookup. If it's an arbitrary string from web (e.g., custom ifName), command injection via `;` in the ifname → system(RCE). Similarly gateway/ip are numeric (swIpAddr2Str from in_addr) → not injectable.\n\nBUT the reachability of this from network input: uclited receives config via IPC from httpd after login. It's the same \"web→system\" class. Which httpd function calls the equivalent \"set\" path? In httpd there are swSetStaticRouteCfg? Hmm — actually the flow: httpd writes route config to uclited via ucMsg (system message) — uclited's uc interface: \"ucStaticRouteCfg\" (referenced in ExecuteStaticRouteTable: ucStaticRouteCfg_getSize()). The data origin: web form → httpd (auth) → IPC → uclited → ExecuteStaticRouteTable.\n\nCommand injection on ifname (a1+16): where does a1+16 come from? Route table entry struct: a1: [0]=dest ip, [4]=netmask, [8]=gateway, [12]=flag?, [16]=ifname string. The ifname field is a string within the route entry that came from the web/IPC buffer. If the web sends \"ifname\" free-form... In TP-Link's newer UI there IS a \"接口\" text field? Not sure. This is the classic CVE family: TP-Link 命令注入 in uclited via 静态路由接口名 (e.g., CVE-2019-17622? no that was a different one...). Actually yes: **CVE-2018-14335**?? Hmm, TP-Link Archer series \"command injection in uclited via 'Interface' field of the static route page\" — the well-known one is CVE-2018-... for TL-WR841N and Archer via \"interface name\" — I recall \"TEW-…\". Let me not rely on CVEs; the code shows sprintf+system with ifname embedded. If ifname is injectable → vuln.\n\nLet me find where route entries are filled from IPC: search functions with \"StaticRoute\" in uclited: we have getStaticRouteTblSize, ExecuteStaticRouteTable, setIfNameDhcpStaticRoute, saveDhcpStaticRoute (0x4400ec), executeDhcpStaticRoute, flushDhcpStaticRoute, setDhcpStaticRoute. And in httpd side — the web page StaticRouteTableRpm.htm / VirtualServer etc. handlers call uc* IPC sets. The static route config path: httpd reads form fields incl. \"interface\"/\"destip\"/\"netmask\"/\"gateway\" → builds a route entry → sends to uclited (via shared mem \"sysvar\"?) . Under the hood it's \"sendMsgToHwNat\" / ucStaticRouteCfg... \n\nFor evidence-based confirmation, I need to trace the ifname value from the web input to a1+16. Let me look at httpd side route config functions: search \"StaticRoute\" in httpd; and \"swSetStaticRoute\"? Let me search both binaries.\n\nAlso check saveDhcpStaticRoute (0x4400ec) & setIfNameDhcpStaticRoute (0x43fffc) — the latter is literally \"Set IfName Dhcp Static Route\" — ifname from DHCP! It probably parses ifname from a string (e.g., from /tmp/resolv or dhcp lease) and passes","stream":true,"block":0}

