data: {"seq":358,"ts":"2026-08-22T16:49:25.522Z","text":"sub_533F8C is the DynDNS HTTP client builder — 609 lines with many sprintf into `haystack` buffer (512 bytes from the thread). It builds an HTTP request with user-supplied fields (hostname, wildcard, mx, backmx, offline...). It writes into haystack with **sprintf without bounds checks**: `v14 = &haystack[sprintf(haystack, \"GET %s?\", v10)];` etc. — haystack is only **512 bytes**. a3 = config (g_dyn_config[142*a1]) — fields: a3+4 = username?, a3+68 = password, a3+... hostname...\n\nIf the \"hostname\" field (from web DDNS page, e.g., \"DDNS name\" for dyn.com) is long, sprintf writes overflow haystack[512] → **stack/heap buffer overflow**? haystack = &unk_5CFEC4 + 512*a1 — a global array (unk_5CFEC4, sized 512*max_wan) — writing beyond 512 bytes overflows into the next WAN slot global → heap-ish global overflow; and sprintf returns advanced pointers cumulatively without checking against 512. Also string fields bounded? The config fields were bounded by strncpy (0x3F) at the web handler — so hostname ≤ 63 bytes, username ≤ 63,","stream":true,"block":0}

