data: {"seq":385,"ts":"2026-08-22T16:49:37.763Z","text":"KEY INSIGHT — this sub_533F8C has **bounds checks that prevent the buffer overflow**:\n\nEvery sprintf is guarded: before writing, it computes the would-be end pointer and compares `(unsigned int)&xxx[...] >= 0x64FEC4` → error return. 0x64FEC4 is the END of the global haystack area (unk_5CFEC4 + 512*N). So the DDNS request builder is **bounds-checked against the end of the global buffer** — the \"overflow\" is prevented (it just aborts with \"error in File:dynDdns.c Line:xxx\"). So NOT exploitable → good thing I read the full function! (The earlier brief preview was misleading.)\n\nNote also: `recv(v83, haystack, 0x200, 0)` — recv into haystack 512 bytes, fine.\n\nAlso `sprintf(s, \"%s:%s\", a3+4, a3+68)` → s[132]; user 63 + ':' + pass 63 = 127 < 132 fits.\n\nSo NO DDNS overflow. Good — avoided a false positive.\n\nOK, so from where does the next CONFIRMED vuln come? I've now spent a lot on httpd. Let me refocus on what remains strongly verifiable and score-driven:\n- The scored P0 was dhcp6s (vsprintf+system - not exploitable) — done.\n- dhcp6s option parsing — bounds-checked mostly; but there's still `case 11` auth handling: `a3[53] = v7 - 31; malloc(v7-31); memcpy(v47, src, a3[53])` — src = v5+11; v7-31 bytes from v5+11: total avail = v7-...(11 + (v7-31) = v7-20 ≤ v7) — fits. OK.\n- uclited — huge; the swGetSystemArpEntries etc. read /proc. Not cmd-inj.\n\nNew idea — **httpd \"wmbSystemLoad\" / getSystemArpEntries** — read `/proc/net/arp`; no exec.\n\nWhat about **sub_405170 (dhcp6s response builder)** — builds the reply into a fixed buffer; it appends options from config and from the request (echo of client DUID etc.). If it snprintf's DUID strings... Let me quickly scan sub_405170 for dangerous calls (brief).\n\nAlso **dhcp6c**: `client6_recv` parses Advertise/Reply; there's a known KAME bug: `dhcp6c` IA_NA address list overflow in `dhcp6_get_options` when multiple IAADDR options (each adds 24 bytes to a fixed array?) — let me check dhcp6c's dhcp6_add_listval (it's a linked list in this codebase — malloc-based, safe).\n\nHonestly, I've done broad coverage. The bpalogin finding stands","stream":true,"block":0}

