data: {"seq":497,"ts":"2026-08-22T16:50:29.299Z","text":"duidcpy: malloc(v2) + memcpy — safe (heap alloc sized by attacker length; only allocation-size issue, no overflow).\n\nI've now done a thorough pass. Honest conclusion for this round:\n\n**No additional confirmable vulnerability found** in this round beyond the already-recorded bpalogin one. The candidates examined were excluded with real evidence. Record FALSE positives would violate discipline.\n\nStill — the user wants a dynamic+static combo continuation. One more dynamic idea that I haven't actually tried yet this round: **httpd trace with a *query-param-bearing GET*** — e.g., request_path=\"/userRpm/DynDdnsRpm.htm?Save=1&wan=0&provider=1&username=admin&pwd=x&cliUrl=...\" — maybe the reset is because httpd processes GET only with Host header/query? No — reset happens identically for bare \"/\". The harness likely sends \"GET / HTTP/1.0\\r\\n\\r\\n\" — httpd (wmnet) may require \"HTTP/1.1\" + Host (sub_40D1FC minidlna rejected without Host; httpd may too → new_conn closes → RST). If httpd closes the connection with RST on missing Host, the harness gives \"Connection reset\" — fully consistent! So httpd needs Host; harness probably can't send headers (request_path only). That explains everything and confirms harness limitation, not a crash.\n\nConclusion: dynamically, this harness cannot exercise httpd at all (needs Host header); cannot exercise UDP; minidlna hangs. Fuzz infra broken. So realistically NO additional dynamic evidence is obtainable this session.\n\nFinal response: \n- State that the round produced no NEW confirmable vuln beyond F-mt4ljkg4-bede (already recorded); that's the correct, discipline-compliant outcome.\n- Provide the exclusions with evidence addresses (DDNS bounds check 0x64FEC4; static-route ifname internal; duidcpy malloc'd; dnsproxy decode within 4096; httpSetEnv alloc-based).\n- Explain the httpd Host-header hypothesis for the reset (evidence: httpd requires Host per send-recv; minidlna's parser explicitly rejects without Host → the family of servers behaves this way; the triggers all reset).\n\nWait — actually, let me reconsider ONE more time whether the httpd reset could be turned into a findable bug via a trace with **argv trick**: httpd supports \"-r\" reset? Not relevant.\n\nHmm, but hold on — maybe I should attempt the minidlnad trace once more now that orchestrator restarted (the RPATH rejection was an old qemu-config issue that the restart may have fixed; the retry that \"hung\" was killed by restart). If minidlna trace now completes with diff on \"/\" → we could observe the URL parse path (sub_40D1FC) dynamically and at least *confirm reachability* of the earlier \"v8[7]\" region... but we classified that as IDA artifact. Reality check: minidlna without config: does it even bind before scanning? It starts start_database fork + scanner; the HTTP server thread runs concurrently. The hang was probably its fork/scanner under qemu... The orchestrator restart KILLED the worker (status failed). So the hang may have been the worker being killed during restart, not minidlna itself. There's a real chance a fresh minidlnad trace completes now. And if it produces a diff (URL handlers newly hit), that's REAL dynamic reachability evidence for minidlnad — no vuln yet confirmed there, but the diff would let me pivot to fuzz... fuzz is broken. So diff alone wouldn't confirm a vuln. Unless the *trigger* crashes minidlna → trigger_result would show error and diff could reveal crash path. Interesting but low probability.\n\nBudget check: traces used ~7 of 12 (2225718d1472, ef55266d1f34, 2ba400f78c44, 8519f7adc93d, 4cfb0cac0b5c, 93b94696d1f5, 157e1f694b18) → 5 left. One minidlna attempt is affordable (~1-3 min).\n\nLet me try minidlna once more (port 8200, \"/\", argv []). If it completes with diff, great (dynamic coverage for DLNA HTTP — then I can look at what got hit; maybe a crash!). If it hangs/fails, final report.","stream":true,"block":0}

