data: {"seq":139,"ts":"2026-08-22T16:54:57.422Z","text":"Still running. Let me wait a bit more, then poll the whole-binary one. Meanwhile — one more targeted static analysis for a *second* vulnerable DB-check: bpalogin had another unbounded `extract_valuestring` into (a1+1150) nonce and (a1+1020), plus (a1+12252) — 3 more unbounded copies of wire length (16-bit up to 65535) into the *config struct* (heap/global, a1 ~ 12780 bytes). Actually (a1+12252) with a 65k value overflows the struct end! a1 size: struct has fields up to 12776 (12764, 12768, 12772, 12776) → ~","stream":true,"block":0}

event: thinking
data: {"seq":140,"ts":"2026-08-22T16:54:57.798Z","text":"Still running. Let me wait a bit more, then poll the whole-binary one. Meanwhile — one more targeted static analysis for a *second* vulnerable DB-check: bpalogin had another unbounded `extract_valuestring` into (a1+1150) nonce and (a1+1020), plus (a1+12252) — 3 more unbounded copies of wire length (16-bit up to 65535) into the *config struct* (heap/global, a1 ~ 12780 bytes). Actually (a1+12252) with a 65k value overflows the struct end! a1 size: struct has fields up to 12776 (12764, 12768, 12772, 12776) → ~12780 bytes. extract_valuestring(a1, v35, 0x09, (char*)(a1+12252)) with v13-4 up to 65531 → writes way past a1+12252 (overflow of the whole struct + heap). That's the *same* bug family as the v34 stack overflow — same trigger","stream":true,"block":0}

event: thinking
data: {"seq":141,"ts":"2026-08-22T16:54:58.191Z","text":"Still running. Let me wait a bit more, then poll the whole-binary one. Meanwhile — one more targeted static analysis for a *second* vulnerable DB-check: bpalogin had another unbounded `extract_valuestring` into (a1+1150) nonce and (a1+1020), plus (a1+12252) — 3 more unbounded copies of wire length (16-bit up to 65535) into the *config struct* (heap/global, a1 ~ 12780 bytes). Actually (a1+12252) with a 65k value overflows the struct end! a1 size: struct has fields up to 12776 (12764, 12768, 12772, 12776) → ~12780 bytes. extract_valuestring(a1, v35, 0x09, (char*)(a1+12252)) with v13-4 up to 65531 → writes way past a1+12252 (overflow of the whole struct + heap). That's the *same* bug family as the v34 stack overflow — same trigger (Login RESP field 9). My recorded finding covers the login() parsing broadly (single vuln class); the record's call_chain + poc already focuses on field 0x16 → v34 stack overflow. I could keep it as ONE finding (same sink function) — that's honest.\n\nH","stream":true,"block":0}

event: thinking
data: {"seq":142,"ts":"2026-08-22T16:54:58.547Z","text":"Still running. Let me wait a bit more, then poll the whole-binary one. Meanwhile — one more targeted static analysis for a *second* vulnerable DB-check: bpalogin had another unbounded `extract_valuestring` into (a1+1150) nonce and (a1+1020), plus (a1+12252) — 3 more unbounded copies of wire length (16-bit up to 65535) into the *config struct* (heap/global, a1 ~ 12780 bytes). Actually (a1+12252) with a 65k value overflows the struct end! a1 size: struct has fields up to 12776 (12764, 12768, 12772, 12776) → ~12780 bytes. extract_valuestring(a1, v35, 0x09, (char*)(a1+12252)) with v13-4 up to 65531 → writes way past a1+12252 (overflow of the whole struct + heap). That's the *same* bug family as the v34 stack overflow — same trigger (Login RESP field 9). My recorded finding covers the login() parsing broadly (single vuln class); the record's call_chain + poc already focuses on field 0x16 → v34 stack overflow. I could keep it as ONE finding (same sink function) — that's honest.\n\nHmm, but maybe I should strengthen: record the finding already done. OK.\n\nWhile waiting — check fz-86cbf814 (whole binary).","stream":true,"block":0}

event: thinking
data: {"seq":143,"ts":"2026-08-22T16:54:58.647Z","text":"Still running. Let me wait a bit more, then poll the whole-binary one. Meanwhile — one more targeted static analysis for a *second* vulnerable DB-check: bpalogin had another unbounded `extract_valuestring` into (a1+1150) nonce and (a1+1020), plus (a1+12252) — 3 more unbounded copies of wire length (16-bit up to 65535) into the *config struct* (heap/global, a1 ~ 12780 bytes). Actually (a1+12252) with a 65k value overflows the struct end! a1 size: struct has fields up to 12776 (12764, 12768, 12772, 12776) → ~12780 bytes. extract_valuestring(a1, v35, 0x09, (char*)(a1+12252)) with v13-4 up to 65531 → writes way past a1+12252 (overflow of the whole struct + heap). That's the *same* bug family as the v34 stack overflow — same trigger (Login RESP field 9). My recorded finding covers the login() parsing broadly (single vuln class); the record's call_chain + poc already focuses on field 0x16 → v34 stack overflow. I could keep it as ONE finding (same sink function) — that's honest.\n\nHmm, but maybe I should strengthen: record the finding already done. OK.\n\nWhile waiting — check fz-86cbf814 (whole binary).","stream":false,"block":0}

